WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.690
- Welcome to the Lifespan podcast,

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:04.630
where we discuss the science of aging

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:06.893
and how to be healthier
at any stage of life.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.690
I'm David Sinclair.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.690
I'm a professor of genetics
at Harvard Medical School,

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.020
and I'm also co-director

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.520
of the Paul F. Glenn Center
for Biology of Aging Research.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:20.870
In this podcast series,

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.667
we're talking about why we age

00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:26.150
and how to slow, stop and
even reverse that process,

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:29.120
to give us longer and healthier lives.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.110
In this final episode of
the eight part series,

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.310
we're talking about the very near future,

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.510
what's coming down the line

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:40.240
that will potentially
greatly extend our lifespans.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.720
I'm joined here by my lovely co-host

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.770
and my co-author of "Lifespan",

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:46.070
Matthew LaPlante, welcome.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:47.023
- Hey, thank you.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.540
- So yeah, we've been talking
over the last few minutes

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.210
about what the future really looks like

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.660
and how much it's going
to change our lives,

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:57.770
but even how hard it is
to predict the future

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:58.620
just a few years out,

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.020
'cause things are changing so quickly.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:05.420
- Well, in fact, we set off
to give some predictions

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.660
when we worked on "Lifespan" together.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.520
And a lot of the things that you had said,

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:15.300
you envisioned coming down
the line maybe five years out,

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.330
maybe 10 years out.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.090
By the time the book had published,

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.740
and certainly now two years
after the book had published,

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.420
they're already here.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.300
- We even have this
illustration in the book

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.320
of a person going online,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.290
doing a telemedicine
interview with a doctor

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.520
and having medicines
delivered to their home,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:32.570
in that case, by drones.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.460
But we're pretty much already
there, thanks to the pandemic.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.710
- Yeah we are and I think
there's a lot of other things

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.270
that are just coming a lot faster

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.140
than we could have even envisioned.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:42.753
And also in part, because of the pandemic,

00:01:42.753 --> 00:01:43.990
because the pandemic

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.910
has really brought so
many of us face to face

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:51.703
with some of the constraints
of how our world was organized

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:57.730
in the before times and how
that it's not very flexible,

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.620
it's not very responsive to the needs

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:05.270
that we are experiencing,
not just in the pandemic,

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:10.270
but also as we reorchestrate
our lives in the 21st century.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.060
- Yeah, and even when we wrote the book,

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.540
there was pushback even
within our conversations,

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.030
how realistic is it that people

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.040
will take their own healthcare,

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.090
medical care into their own hands

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.630
and would they be allowed to?

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:24.710
And we've been rapidly
pushed into to that world,

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:28.080
where you can take home tests
for pretty much anything.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.550
You can have blood tests
for pretty much anything.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.010
Of course, doctors have to be involved,

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:32.843
but you don't have to go see a doctor-

00:02:32.843 --> 00:02:34.430
- You don't have to go
to a doctor's office.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:35.300
- Right, and in the future,

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.370
it's going to be rarer
to go to see your doctor.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.300
Most of it's going to be done remotely

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.610
and you won't even know the doctor

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.880
that's involved in prescribing something.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:43.770
- And thank God, really,

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:47.100
because there's really no
experience people like less

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.600
than going to the doctor.
- Well-

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.010
- If you can do that in
the comfort of your home,

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.610
it's going to be a better experience.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:53.670
- Well, yeah, I was pretty
sure I would get sicker

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.140
when I went to that waiting room

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.610
with people coughing
and sneezing everywhere.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.630
Hospitals are just as
bad, it's pretty risky,

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.080
particularly as an older
person, going to the hospital.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.350
And if you don't need to, if
you can be monitored at home,

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:07.180
which is increasingly possible,

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.550
then a lot of people won't
die from an infection

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.940
that they would typically
get at the hospital.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.150
- You said monitoring,

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:16.440
and I think this is a overarching theme

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.080
of this podcast series so far.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.670
One of the things we've
said again and again,

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:22.700
is that you can't impact
what you don't measure.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.238
And so today, we're going to
talk a lot about wearables

00:03:26.238 --> 00:03:27.620
and implantables,

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:30.190
and we're going to talk
about how these innovations,

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:34.700
along with scaled access
to biological aging clocks

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.610
is going to change the
way we address health.

