00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:50.570
- Okay, so David, let's get into this.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.230
And to do this, to talk about the future,

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.030
we have to talk a little
bit about the past,

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:57.570
we have to talk about the
world we're in right now

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:59.240
when it comes to healthcare.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:04.190
Right now, our healthcare
system is based on a model

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:06.830
that has developed over
the last few hundred years.

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:11.520
And that model basically amounts
to wait till you get sick,

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.590
then go see your doctor.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.010
And when you and I have
talked about this before,

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:18.160
you said that actually
makes plenty of sense

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.270
in the context of the
19th and 20th centuries.

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.780
- Well, it does, because
doctors had no way of knowing

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:24.613
if you were sick,

00:07:24.613 --> 00:07:25.860
other than asking you how you feel,

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.570
and they couldn't do that remotely,

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:28.940
certainly not through video,

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:30.350
but they also needed to feel you

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.483
and to take samples in the office.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:35.130
Right now, you can wear a biosensor,

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:38.840
a monitor or do a blood test
from home or a local clinic,

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:41.110
that will give the doctor
way more information

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:43.620
than they could ever get
by seeing you in person.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:47.200
So a case in point, I saw my
doctor maybe six months ago

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.439
and I hadn't seen him for a year

00:07:49.439 --> 00:07:50.520
and it was a video conference,

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.720
and it was supposed to be
very quick, 10 minutes.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:54.967
He said, "David, how do you feel?"

00:07:54.967 --> 00:07:55.800
"I'm feeling good."

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:56.633
"How are you sleeping?"

00:07:56.633 --> 00:07:57.466
"Not so good."

00:07:57.466 --> 00:07:58.557
"Well, you should get more sleep,

00:07:58.557 --> 00:08:01.347
"try to not do your emails too late.

00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:02.317
"Anything else you want to talk about?"

00:08:02.317 --> 00:08:03.907
And I said, "Yeah, there's a lot.

00:08:03.907 --> 00:08:06.887
"I'm, you know, 52, I
could have colon cancer,

00:08:06.887 --> 00:08:08.537
"I could have all these other issues.

00:08:08.537 --> 00:08:10.030
"Let's do some tests."

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.330
And he says, "Well, okay, what
kind of tests do you want?"

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:15.667
So I said, "What about
a prostate cancer test?

00:08:15.667 --> 00:08:18.207
"You know, for my age, I
could have prostate cancer.

00:08:18.207 --> 00:08:21.690
"My neighbor just died
from prostate cancer."

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:24.057
And he said, "Well, do you
have a family history?"

00:08:24.057 --> 00:08:25.657
"Not that I know of."

00:08:25.657 --> 00:08:27.037
"Do you feel sick?"

00:08:27.037 --> 00:08:27.870
"No."

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:29.320
He goes, "Well, then there's
no need to do the test."

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:32.247
I said, "Hold it right
there, hold it right there.

00:08:32.247 --> 00:08:33.267
"Are you telling me

00:08:33.267 --> 00:08:37.567
"that I need to have a
large tumor in my prostate

00:08:37.567 --> 00:08:39.397
"that's causing me to be sick,
before I come and see you?"

00:08:39.397 --> 00:08:41.490
And he said, "Yeah."
- Yes, right.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:42.381
- Yes.

00:08:42.381 --> 00:08:43.214
That's craziness.
- And that's model

00:08:43.214 --> 00:08:44.994
of medicine in the 20th century, right?

00:08:44.994 --> 00:08:45.880
- And then, it's often too late,

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:47.420
then I'm on my way to death.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:50.260
So these days, there are
tests that you can do

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:51.920
that your doctor can order.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:54.210
There's a new one that's
just about to be released

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:57.650
by a company called GRAIL
and there are many others,

00:08:57.650 --> 00:08:58.870
that, with a finger prick

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:01.700
or a little blood test
from a local clinic,

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:03.700
you can detect 50
different types of cancers

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:06.160
before they even become tumors

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.580
and kill them years
before they would show up

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:10.920
and have you go to a doctor,
which is often too late.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:12.010
- Let's talk about why that is,

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:17.010
because when a cancer cell starts to form,

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:19.210
when a tumor starts to form,

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:20.680
it begins releasing

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:25.203
into the bloodstream these
signalers, blood analytes.

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:29.720
And if we can detect those,

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:33.390
we can detect cancer at the
very, very earliest stages.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:34.223
- Well, that's right,

00:09:34.223 --> 00:09:35.900
and they give off a lot
of different signals.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:38.260
Out of the breath and in the bloodstream,

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:41.710
they leach proteins, they leach DNA, RNA,

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:43.380
and even the types of DNA that come out,

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:46.400
those little fragments,
they have little ends,

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:47.950
it's not the whole
chromosome that comes out,

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:50.410
they get chopped up, the cancer cells die,

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:52.080
and out come these little pieces.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.690
Those pieces have chemicals
on them, the DNA methylation,

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:57.670
the clock that can be read,

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:58.700
and even at the ends,

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:00.550
how they were cut up
by enzymes in the cell,

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:02.420
can tell you, indeed,

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:06.620
that is a stage one liver cancer,

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:08.690
even though we picked it
up in the bloodstream.

00:10:08.690 --> 00:10:12.070
- Which means we don't now need to wait

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:17.070
for a tumor to grow, to metastasize,

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.360
for it to spread into
other systems in the body.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:23.120
There is increasingly a chance

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:25.780
of catching these kinds
of problems earlier.

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:26.730
And it's not just cancer,

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:28.720
it's a lot of diseases, heart disease,

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:30.170
which is not a cancer, right?

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:32.410
Also, there are signs of heart disease

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:35.850
that you can pick up through
these early warning signals.

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:37.340
- Exactly, so with the cancer,

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:40.530
we've been slowly reducing
the incidence of cancer

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:42.610
and particularly the
ability to survive cancer

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:44.370
and we haven't cured cancer,

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:45.760
but it's gotten a lot better,

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:47.110
particularly for diseases

00:10:47.110 --> 00:10:49.760
like breast cancer, lung cancer, too.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.040
What I'm excited about is
that this new development

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:53.960
that's not come from oncologists,

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:56.650
but from people who develop these tests,

00:10:56.650 --> 00:10:58.790
the geneticists that can find blood,

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:00.883
what's called circulating DNA.

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:02.863
This is going

00:11:02.863 --> 00:11:05.760
to dramatically reduce
the incidence of cancer

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:06.930
and certainly the death rate.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:07.763
Why?

00:11:07.763 --> 00:11:09.770
Because if you catch a
tumor when it's small,

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:11.150
you take some chemotherapy

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:13.610
and you kill it off before
it spreads and it's too late.

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:14.443
So that's cancer.

00:11:14.443 --> 00:11:15.276
Heart disease.

00:11:15.276 --> 00:11:17.120
Heart disease you can
measure through blood tests,

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:18.930
so we do InsideTracker,

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:20.760
you can see that there
are inflammatory markers

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.390
that predict cardiovascular disease.

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:25.900
But also, what's coming along,

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:27.530
that'll be added to those blood tests

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:30.400
is the ability to measure
your heart's function.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.223
So I wear a device that's right here,

00:11:34.747 --> 00:11:35.800
this one's called a BioButton,

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:37.520
you can see it's about an inch.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:39.820
- You know you want to
take off your shirt.

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:40.850
- Do you want me to take off my shirt?

00:11:40.850 --> 00:11:43.370
- I mean, I don't personally need you

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:44.240
to take off your shirt,

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:46.630
I just know that you've been
waiting for the opportunity

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.130
to take off your shirt on camera.

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:49.900
- That is not true, but I can show...

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:51.980
- He's been waiting for the opportunity,

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:54.450
he's shy now, but he wants to do it.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:55.350
It's like Iron Man.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:56.183
- We don't need to talk me taking-

00:11:56.183 --> 00:11:57.520
- You have like a little device

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:58.520
right in the middle of your chest.

00:11:58.520 --> 00:11:59.353
What is this thing?

00:11:59.353 --> 00:12:02.240
Okay, tell me what is this
thing monitoring right now?

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:04.640
- Well, so instead of going
to your doctor once a year

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:05.520
for an annual checkup,

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.310
this thing's monitoring my
body 1,000 times a second

00:12:08.310 --> 00:12:09.143
for vibration.

00:12:09.143 --> 00:12:11.410
It can hear how I speak, how I cough,

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:12.730
it's looking at my movements,

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:15.020
how I sleep during the day, how I move.

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:16.520
But most importantly, for this device,

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.850
it's measuring my heart
in many different ways.

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:20.613
It's actually an FDA approved EKG,

00:12:20.613 --> 00:12:21.470
this is something

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:23.860
that you have to put
stickers all over your body,

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:25.020
the doctor used to measure it,

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:25.910
sine waves and whatever,

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:27.330
you can now do it with your wristwatch,

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:29.090
though that's not FDA approved.

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:30.530
This one is the real deal.

00:12:30.530 --> 00:12:33.390
Doctors use this information
to diagnose patients.

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:35.570
And what's it's being used for mainly now,

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:37.310
is to send patients home early,

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:38.600
who have experienced a heart attack

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:40.120
or have had heart surgery,

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:42.160
just to see if they're
doing all right at home

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:45.220
and hospitals save tens of
thousands of dollars that way,

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:47.250
for something that only costs about $20.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:48.810
- But why wait for somebody
to have a heart attack,

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:50.480
and then send 'em home with one,

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:52.350
if you can put them on people

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:54.570
who are at risk of heart attack,

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:56.220
or even before then they

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:58.870
become at acute risk
of heart attack, right?

00:12:58.870 --> 00:13:01.690
I mean, like if we can start
monitoring people early on,

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:03.800
figure out what their normal rhythm is,

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:08.330
be attentive when that
rhythm starts to falter,

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:10.350
we can catch things really, really early.

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:11.183
We will.

00:13:11.183 --> 00:13:12.340
There's a future already that's here,

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:15.400
that this device will
send an note to my doctor

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:17.560
or a nurse that's scanning hundreds

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:20.220
and eventually millions of people.

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:21.560
And there'll be a red alert,

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:22.830
it'll be like "Minority Report",

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:24.110
I've seen this actually in development,

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:26.167
you call it up, "Here's David's heart.

00:13:26.167 --> 00:13:28.640
"And the kidneys are
also having an issue."

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:29.890
The doctor then looks at it,

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:32.370
he probably, or she,
is playing a golf game

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.452
or doing something else,
but they can see it.

00:13:34.452 --> 00:13:37.402
They go, "Okay, this person's
about to have a heart attack.

00:13:38.916 --> 00:13:40.170
"Send in the robot."

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:41.950
This might be a molecular dynamics,

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:43.230
like Lex Fridman's robots.

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:45.070
- Okay, yeah, and I want
to talk about the future,

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:47.780
but I think we need to
first sort of, like,

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:51.090
set the stage for what's
actually going on right now,

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:53.490
because it's not just heart monitors.

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:55.730
That thing is not monitoring your blood,

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:59.000
but you're also wearing,
and I'm wearing also,

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:03.070
something that is actually
actively monitoring our blood.

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:05.603
- Yeah, this is the Levels
continuous glucose monitor,

00:14:05.603 --> 00:14:07.053
it's on our arms here.

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:10.500
And we've been playing
with this the last week.

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:11.680
Together, we've been competing

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:14.300
to see who's got the
lower blood sugar levels.

00:14:14.300 --> 00:14:16.400
And we just measured it
before we went on air

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:17.660
and we're identical.

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:18.493
So that's-

00:14:18.493 --> 00:14:21.627
- You're winning, like, by a
percentage point right now.

00:14:21.627 --> 00:14:22.930
- Right, but we're both healthy,

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:25.260
and what we've learned
by eating meals together

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:27.030
over this past episode,

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:30.010
is that what we eat really
makes a big difference.

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:32.440
So things like rice and grapes,

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:33.913
for me, really spike it,

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:36.410
potatoes, not so much.

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:38.610
Have you learned anything
with this device?

00:14:38.610 --> 00:14:42.980
- I feel like I've learned
more about blood sugar

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:44.580
in a week of wearing this device,

00:14:44.580 --> 00:14:47.770
than I knew about in my
entire life before then.

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:52.420
Now, I should say, I'm not
diabetic, I'm not prediabetic,

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:56.950
so really, like, under the sort
of the traditional paradigm

00:14:56.950 --> 00:15:00.180
of how we diagnose and treat diseases,

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:03.970
there was no reason for me to
know what my blood sugar is.

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:07.290
But I'm realizing now, like, honestly,

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:09.350
like realizing now that,
like, there is a reason,

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:12.490
and the reason why is it
fundamentally changes the way

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:15.170
that I think about the foods
that I'm putting into my body,

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:17.270
because I'm now thinking
about it in context

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:19.147
of, "Will this do something to me

00:15:19.147 --> 00:15:20.700
"that I know is not very good for me?"

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:22.070
Which is spike my blood sugar.

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:22.903
- Yeah, exactly.

00:15:22.903 --> 00:15:26.320
And it's similar to
scales in your bathroom.

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:27.730
It's knowing that if you're told

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:29.210
to weigh yourself every morning,

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:31.760
you will lose weight more often than not.

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:34.340
And I liken these two to scales,

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:35.880
we can measure our bodies.

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:36.713
I mean, who's to say,

00:15:36.713 --> 00:15:38.420
we're not allowed to
look inside our bodies.

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:39.840
There are doctors who don't believe

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:41.499
that these should be allowed
to be freely available.

00:15:41.499 --> 00:15:42.332
- Well, in fact,

00:15:42.332 --> 00:15:44.110
you have to have a prescription
for this right now.

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:45.530
- You do, you do.

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:48.000
And, you know, until
further studies are showing

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:50.950
that they're safe, you know,
that's a reasonable thing.

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:53.010
But I do believe that
people have the right

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:56.810
to look inside their bodies
and we can interpret the data.

00:15:56.810 --> 00:15:59.520
- And shouldn't wait until
they're diseased to do so.

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:00.353
- Exactly.

00:16:00.353 --> 00:16:03.630
So right now, there are some
in the medical establishment

00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:06.390
that think that these should
only be used for diabetics.

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:08.960
My argument is are bathroom
scales only for those

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:10.420
who are morbidly obese?

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:13.540
No, anybody can use them to
tell more about their bodies

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:15.280
and how they can improve.

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:17.730
- Wearables have come a really long way.

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:20.843
So this is a blood glucose monitor.

00:16:23.618 --> 00:16:26.560
Sort of, like, there's
a long and rich history

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:29.370
of blood glucose monitors,

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:32.180
these were sort of the first
wearables to come along,

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:34.940
and the first at home test
that people could use.

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:37.140
- Right, the history's pretty interesting.

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:40.350
The first ability to
measure glucose in blood

00:16:40.350 --> 00:16:42.410
was way back, 1962.

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:43.690
It wasn't until 1973,

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:47.510
that there was a desktop or
more like a bench top analyzer,

00:16:47.510 --> 00:16:48.530
it was more like a fridge.

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:49.450
- This thing was huge.

00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:50.283
- It was massive.

00:16:50.283 --> 00:16:51.116
But no, so 1973,

00:16:51.116 --> 00:16:53.430
you could finally send
in a sample of blood

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:54.960
and have your glucose measured.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:56.380
And that's how it's been done

00:16:56.380 --> 00:17:00.110
for really up until
these devices came along.

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:01.640
Increasingly, we're now seeing wearables

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:02.490
being able to do it.

00:17:02.490 --> 00:17:06.750
Watches, these semi-invasive needles.

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:07.930
Eventually what we're going to have,

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:11.270
is the ability to incorporate
it into one of these stickers

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:14.420
and not just one
measurement, but hundreds.

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:16.280
- And that's really where things start

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:19.590
to get very informative
and very interesting,

00:17:19.590 --> 00:17:20.580
because what you can do

00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:24.610
is now you can decide based
on your genetic profile,

00:17:24.610 --> 00:17:26.560
your propensity for disease,

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:28.140
perhaps even the way

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:31.440
that your genes have been
methylated over time,

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:35.260
you can decide what the biomarkers are

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:36.093
that you need to know.

00:17:36.093 --> 00:17:38.070
And you can do this in
consultation with your doctor,

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:38.980
another advisor

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:41.377
who understands these
things, you know, best.

00:17:41.377 --> 00:17:43.007
And you can say, "Okay,
what I want to watch

00:17:43.007 --> 00:17:44.420
"is for this and this and this."

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:45.340
Right now, you can look at...

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:46.270
Pretty much,

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:49.010
lots of people have access
now to blood glucose,

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:50.150
but we're going to be able to look

00:17:50.150 --> 00:17:53.590
at all sorts of other blood biomarkers.

00:17:53.590 --> 00:17:54.920
- Yeah, there's an interesting point

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:58.320
that it's kind of the end of
the story about my doctor.

00:17:58.320 --> 00:17:59.760
Even though my doctor was reticent

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:01.120
and eventually capitulated

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:03.460
in giving me that prostate cancer test,

00:18:03.460 --> 00:18:06.030
thank you, Dr. Peter.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:06.863
- And you're all good?

00:18:06.863 --> 00:18:07.696
- I'm good, yeah, right?
- Yeah.

00:18:07.696 --> 00:18:09.880
- And it's very simple
test called the PSA test,

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:10.780
if you're wondering.

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:11.640
But what's happening

00:18:11.640 --> 00:18:12.940
is it's not that he's a bad doctor,

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:14.820
it's that the medical system is not set up

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:17.570
to give tests willy nilly to
people that aren't yet sick.

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:18.420
And that has to change.
- Well, they're not covered

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:19.780
by most people's insurance,

00:18:19.780 --> 00:18:21.430
unless you reach that threshold.

00:18:21.430 --> 00:18:22.950
Again, like this whole system, right?

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:25.177
Like, the insurance system says,

00:18:25.177 --> 00:18:26.197
"Yes, we will pay for it,

00:18:26.197 --> 00:18:28.000
"but only once your diseased."

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:29.250
Which is as backwards.

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:30.083
- It is.

00:18:30.083 --> 00:18:31.320
And there are countries
that have realized this.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:33.790
So Australia provides sunblock on beaches,

00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:36.840
they also provide free colon cancer tests,

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:39.120
you just poop and send it off.

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:41.280
Everyone in Australia gets
a free colon cancer test.

00:18:41.280 --> 00:18:43.740
That saves billions of
dollars down the line.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:44.900
Today, in the US,

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:47.240
pretty much you have to pay
for a lot of this yourself,

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:49.430
but a lot of people
realize that it's worth it.

00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:50.500
And getting back to my doctor,

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:52.457
what he said was,

00:18:52.457 --> 00:18:55.067
"Oh, you have all this
data from InsideTracker.

00:18:55.067 --> 00:18:56.240
"Let's look at it."

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:59.070
So I call up on the screen all
the data and he's loving it.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.980
So doctors, at least in
the case of my doctor,

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:03.550
he's not upset that I'm measuring myself.

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:05.620
In fact, he would do it for everybody

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:07.070
if the insurance would pay for it.

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:07.950
- Yeah, yeah.

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:10.310
So I also have a very old school doctor

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:13.130
and I love him to death,

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:14.800
I'm going to actually
go see him next week.

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:17.130
I'm really looking forward

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:20.733
to bringing my chart
to see him, because...

00:19:20.733 --> 00:19:22.160
- From the Levels data?

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:23.510
- From the Levels data, yeah,

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:24.840
because I think that this is going

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:26.370
to inform our conversation

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:29.230
in a way that we've never had before.

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:30.063
- Well, exactly.

00:19:30.063 --> 00:19:31.790
And it's just the beginning

00:19:31.790 --> 00:19:35.470
of being cognizant of
that your body matters

00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:37.240
and is changing all the time,

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:38.570
and there are days and months

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:40.980
where it's going to be way out of whack

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:43.037
and you wouldn't know unless
you were measuring it.

00:19:43.037 --> 00:19:46.210
And it's all about, as we
wrote in "Lifespan", the book,

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:47.500
you need a dashboard for your body

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:49.340
in the same way you need
a dashboard for your car.

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:51.370
You wouldn't drive your
car without a dashboard.

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:53.540
Well, you could, but you
wouldn't get very far,

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:55.000
you'd run out of gas.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:56.020
We need that for our bodies,

00:19:56.020 --> 00:19:58.130
and it's really time that we do that.

00:19:58.130 --> 00:20:00.350
And what we'll have is a massive reduction

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:04.230
in diseases like all cancers,
but colon cancer already.

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:05.600
We're going to have heart attacks

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:07.130
be a thing of the past pretty much,

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:09.420
'cause your doctor will
know days in advance

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:11.640
before you actually have a cardiac event.

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:14.790
And other things, even
including depression, anxiety,

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:16.550
these can also be measured by the devices

00:20:16.550 --> 00:20:17.800
that we have on our bodies

00:20:17.800 --> 00:20:20.300
or even in our hands
or the ones we type on.

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:22.560
These indicate diseases.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:23.410
Speaking of typing,

00:20:23.410 --> 00:20:25.350
you can tell if someone's
getting Alzheimer's

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:27.040
or Parkinson's just by how they type.

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.350
- Also by their movements
around the house,

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:31.450
there are systems that are doing that now.

00:20:31.450 --> 00:20:34.140
Again, before we get too futuristic,

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:35.199
this is one of the things I really like-

00:20:35.199 --> 00:20:36.270
- I want to get there.
- About our conversations,

00:20:36.270 --> 00:20:37.960
You're always like, "Let's
talk about the future."

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:40.870
But I really want to talk about
what's available right now,

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:44.650
because right now, if someone wants it,

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:48.680
there are a variety of products

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:51.220
that take a variety of measurements.

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:53.470
There are glucose monitors
like we're wearing,

00:20:53.470 --> 00:20:56.630
there are lactate monitors,

00:20:56.630 --> 00:20:58.900
there are body fat, muscle mass,

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:00.810
body fat percentage monitors,

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:05.810
ECGs, caloric intake,
temperature, UV exposure,

00:21:06.060 --> 00:21:08.243
sleep quality, blood oxygen.

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:11.400
Those are the things that are
just on the market right now

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:12.837
that you, like, just type into Google,

00:21:12.837 --> 00:21:13.960
"I want this kind of monitor."

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:14.810
You can get it.

00:21:14.810 --> 00:21:17.650
What are the, like, five biomarkers

00:21:17.650 --> 00:21:19.150
that you would want to have?

00:21:19.150 --> 00:21:21.077
If I just said, "You can
only have five this week.

00:21:21.077 --> 00:21:22.380
"What are the five that you would want?"

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:24.570
- Well, I'm already
measuring a lot of those.

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:25.673
So glucose-
- But you're doing it

00:21:25.673 --> 00:21:26.506
with InsideTracker,

00:21:26.506 --> 00:21:29.670
which is a regular, like, what?

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:30.642
Monthly or bi-monthly-

00:21:30.642 --> 00:21:31.475
- It's not constant, that's true.

00:21:31.475 --> 00:21:32.750
- Yeah, so what would you want to be able

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:34.110
to monitor constantly right now?

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:35.970
So I would want one of these devices

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:37.860
to not just tell me glucose.

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:40.250
I want it to include heart function,

00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:42.050
which I'm doing as well.

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:43.640
But I also would include inflammation.

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:46.460
So there's a molecule called CRP,

00:21:46.460 --> 00:21:48.010
which I always want to keep low,

00:21:48.010 --> 00:21:49.310
'cause that's a really good predictor

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:50.750
of cardiovascular disease.

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:52.050
- CRP is?

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:53.233
- C-reactive protein.

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:56.040
And so we've got glucose,
C-reactive protein.

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:59.950
Inflammation, so this would
be TNF alpha or IL one, six,

00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:02.600
these are also predictive of longevity.

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:05.610
I want cortisol, keep
my stress levels down,

00:22:05.610 --> 00:22:08.140
or also telling me if I overexercise.

00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:11.160
- I would love to see your cortisol chart.

00:22:11.160 --> 00:22:13.430
- I have it all, I've got
10 years of data, dude.

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:15.220
- Like, no, I would
love to see your, like,

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:16.510
minute by minute cortisol chart.

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:20.690
- Oh, well, yeah, hanging out
with you raises it for sure.

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:24.290
- And then, I think that's four.

00:22:24.290 --> 00:22:26.220
The fifth one, let's see.

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:28.640
I would like to know...

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:29.740
Lactate would be great,

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:32.340
because that would tell me if my body

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:36.200
is able to deal with exercise adequately,

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:37.450
and maybe blood oxygen, too.

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:38.630
So when I'm exercising,

00:22:38.630 --> 00:22:40.350
making sure that I get hypoxic,

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:41.620
which, as we've talked about,

00:22:41.620 --> 00:22:45.610
is one of those adversity
mimetics that turns on your body.

00:22:45.610 --> 00:22:46.940
Well, it is an adversity,

00:22:46.940 --> 00:22:49.270
but it's a hormesis that doesn't kill you,

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:51.220
but makes your body
stronger in the long run.

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:54.130
- In the very near future,

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:58.620
we're going to have wearables
and semi-implantables,

00:22:58.620 --> 00:22:59.700
by which I mean like things

00:22:59.700 --> 00:23:01.170
that maybe stick a small needle

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:02.930
into your arm very painlessly,

00:23:02.930 --> 00:23:04.950
like the Level sensor does.

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:09.330
In the sort of still
fairly near-term future,

00:23:09.330 --> 00:23:12.960
but a little further out, nano biosensors.

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:14.710
- Right, and they're being developed,

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:17.160
I can already get those from companies.

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:18.440
They're not yet commercially available

00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:19.940
and I've tried these out.

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:22.630
This one will measure a variety
of things through the skin,

00:23:22.630 --> 00:23:25.220
including analytes, glucose eventually.

00:23:25.220 --> 00:23:27.410
There are companies that
are, including Levels,

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:30.010
that are going to be
measuring hundreds of things,

00:23:30.010 --> 00:23:32.340
that include the things that I mentioned.

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:33.870
These nanosensors they use,

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:37.540
they don't just use
these chemical reactions,

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:39.893
they'll have antibodies
that can detect proteins,

00:23:39.893 --> 00:23:43.290
that can detect DNA, methylated DNA,

00:23:43.290 --> 00:23:46.370
and they'll be implanted,
eventually, under the skin.

00:23:46.370 --> 00:23:49.040
So they may last for months,
you just recharge them,

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:51.490
put one of those coils on the hand,

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:53.670
maybe for an hour, who
knows, five minutes,

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:55.210
you've recharged this
thing and you're good to go

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:57.080
and they could stay in you for life.

00:23:57.080 --> 00:23:58.860
And we were discussing this earlier,

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:02.300
we had got into a bit of an
argument before we went on air

00:24:02.300 --> 00:24:04.480
and maybe you want to hold me back,

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:05.840
because this is a longer discussion.

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:08.930
But I think that everybody

00:24:08.930 --> 00:24:12.310
should have a biosensor in
their body at every age,

00:24:12.310 --> 00:24:14.223
so that parents, doctors,

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:17.510
can monitor how people are doing

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:21.130
and make better decisions
about food and lifestyle.

00:24:21.130 --> 00:24:22.810
- Well, and the thing is,

00:24:22.810 --> 00:24:26.870
is that we are very quickly
going to be entering a world

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:29.160
in which there is so much data

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:30.870
that we're gathering by ourselves,

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:33.240
that it's just going to
become incomprehensible

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:35.040
to process by itself,

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:37.680
and there's going to be a lot of noise,

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:38.860
the signals are going to be hidden,

00:24:38.860 --> 00:24:41.900
we're going to need AI to
help us understand this,

00:24:41.900 --> 00:24:42.733
which means

00:24:42.733 --> 00:24:45.990
that it's not just about
wearing the monitor 24, seven,

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:48.130
it's also about where the data goes.

00:24:48.130 --> 00:24:49.560
This is the real concern

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:51.690
that we were having
this conversation about.

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:52.550
And what that means,

00:24:52.550 --> 00:24:56.040
is that it's going to have to be
uploaded to a cloud somewhere,

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:58.630
to, presumably, a company.

00:24:58.630 --> 00:24:59.463
I don't think most people

00:24:59.463 --> 00:25:01.410
want a government doing this for them.

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:03.140
Although, in some countries,

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:05.940
we know that's probably
going to be the case.

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:08.920
We're starting to tread into a future

00:25:08.920 --> 00:25:10.160
that I think a lot of people

00:25:10.160 --> 00:25:12.840
are rightfully a little bit nervous about

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:13.880
or a lot nervous about.

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:15.640
- Well, it's going to be a company,

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:17.990
the government is not
developing these things,

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:20.530
and there are many companies
doing this, small and large.

00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:24.100
We know Amazon and Google, Apple,

00:25:24.100 --> 00:25:25.610
they have their health divisions.

00:25:25.610 --> 00:25:27.950
But there are a lot of little
start-ups developing devices

00:25:27.950 --> 00:25:29.040
that may be acquired

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:32.513
or may become the next healthcare giant.

00:25:33.640 --> 00:25:37.240
It is a problem though,
that this data is massive.

00:25:37.240 --> 00:25:40.440
There's terabytes of data
that I'm collecting on my body

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:42.444
and multiply that by a
billion or so people,

00:25:42.444 --> 00:25:43.630
that's going to be a lot of data.

00:25:43.630 --> 00:25:45.280
First of all, where do you saw it?

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:46.520
Who owns it?

00:25:46.520 --> 00:25:47.630
What will be done with it?

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:48.720
Is it anonymized?

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:49.780
Is it under the HIPA act,

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:50.830
hopefully, in America,

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:53.360
that will protect it from
being used against you.

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:55.220
I was on a podcast with Lex Fridman

00:25:55.220 --> 00:25:56.660
and he brought up a
really important point,

00:25:56.660 --> 00:25:59.390
which was we have to
have these rules in place

00:25:59.390 --> 00:26:01.450
before we start giving
all of this data away.

00:26:01.450 --> 00:26:02.450
And he would want,

00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:04.270
one of the main things is
that you have the right

00:26:04.270 --> 00:26:06.920
to delete your data if you choose to.

00:26:06.920 --> 00:26:08.360
And I don't think we're there yet,

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:10.600
a lot of these agreements
don't include that,

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:11.470
but that would be something

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:13.680
that we should definitely
start a conversation about.

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:15.530
I'm in touch with some senators

00:26:15.530 --> 00:26:17.360
and Congress people about that.

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:18.400
This is a world that's coming,

00:26:18.400 --> 00:26:20.180
it's not a question of if, it's just when,

00:26:20.180 --> 00:26:21.680
and it's only in the next few years,

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.690
that these rules should be put in place.

00:26:23.690 --> 00:26:26.630
- Yeah, I mean, nobody should
be waiting to talk about this,

00:26:26.630 --> 00:26:30.100
this is an important thing
that we need to get right,

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:32.280
and we need to get it
right really quickly,

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:35.020
because the technology's not waiting

00:26:35.020 --> 00:26:36.970
for the laws to catch up.

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:40.070
One of the other things
that we should mention

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:43.250
is, particularly when we're
talking about genetic data,

00:26:43.250 --> 00:26:46.950
when you consent as an individual

00:26:46.950 --> 00:26:48.950
to release your genetic data,

00:26:48.950 --> 00:26:50.940
you're not just making a personal choice,

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:54.010
you're actually making
a choice for your family

00:26:54.010 --> 00:26:56.790
who share genetic data with you,

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:59.193
your parents, your children,

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:02.800
this is not just a personal decision.

00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:06.120
- Yeah, so every time one of
your relatives puts their DNA

00:27:06.120 --> 00:27:10.030
and their genetic data into
a database, it affects you.

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:11.705
- Yeah, it's not just their information,

00:27:11.705 --> 00:27:12.990
it's your information, too.

00:27:12.990 --> 00:27:16.390
- Yeah, and so one of the
uses of this, obviously,

00:27:16.390 --> 00:27:18.010
is that your family
learns about their health,

00:27:18.010 --> 00:27:19.740
but another aspect

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:22.160
is that you can use it
to solve cold cases.

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:24.800
In the case of the Golden State Killer,

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:27.060
a case where there was a man, for decades,

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:28.580
going around murdering people,

00:27:28.580 --> 00:27:31.940
13 murders, 50 rapes, 150 burglaries.

00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:34.700
They finally found a
hair from this individual

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:36.640
that didn't have the roots,
but there wasn't much DNA,

00:27:36.640 --> 00:27:39.650
but Ed Green and his team
at a company in Australia

00:27:39.650 --> 00:27:40.800
that I work with,

00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:43.150
they were able to get
enough DNA out of that hair

00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:46.540
to track him and find a relative of him

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:47.560
in one of these databases.

00:27:47.560 --> 00:27:48.630
- Using one of these public databases,

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:51.140
where the relative had
freely, voluntarily,

00:27:51.140 --> 00:27:52.460
given their information.

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:54.640
- Right, and triangulating other aspects

00:27:54.640 --> 00:27:55.990
that they knew about this individual.

00:27:55.990 --> 00:27:57.360
They said, "Ah, this is the guy."

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:58.505
And they arrested him
and he's in jail now.

00:27:58.505 --> 00:28:00.820
- And there's been lots
of police work like this

00:28:00.820 --> 00:28:02.235
in recent years,

00:28:02.235 --> 00:28:03.068
and I think a lot of people look at that

00:28:03.068 --> 00:28:05.057
and they go, "Oh man, that's really great.

00:28:05.057 --> 00:28:06.800
"What a great application."

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:09.970
The same technology used by a government

00:28:09.970 --> 00:28:12.870
that wasn't looking for a
murderer, but a dissident,

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:16.340
or, you know, like a journalist,

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:19.070
could be really, really dangerous.

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:21.430
- Right, so that's why these
laws need to be looked at

00:28:21.430 --> 00:28:23.830
and made sure that they are totally tight

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:26.280
so that they're cannot be a dictator

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:28.040
or some sort of change in government law

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:31.790
that can use this data
for nefarious purposes.

00:28:31.790 --> 00:28:36.330
- The other way that our health
data affects other people

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:39.320
is in infectious diseases.

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:41.780
And so, there's another
question here about, okay,

00:28:41.780 --> 00:28:46.620
so let's say like a
well-meaning government, right?

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:49.350
Identifies a contagious virus, right?

00:28:49.350 --> 00:28:51.320
And we can track these
things now with, you know,

00:28:51.320 --> 00:28:54.610
millions of people wearing
biomonitors everywhere.

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:56.980
You get a spike in one of these analytes,

00:28:56.980 --> 00:28:59.110
and all of a sudden
whoever is tracking this,

00:28:59.110 --> 00:29:00.060
wherever they are,

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:04.170
knows, "Ah, there's a virus
here, it's spreading."

00:29:04.170 --> 00:29:07.110
And I think we're all a lot more cognizant

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:08.470
of, like, what this kind of looks like

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:09.940
and feels like in the era of COVID,

00:29:09.940 --> 00:29:13.460
but, you know, what if it
was a disease like ebola,

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:15.650
not COVID, which is, by itself,

00:29:15.650 --> 00:29:18.720
very, very dangerous and very
deadly for a lot of people,

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:22.377
but what if it's a virus that's
deadly for almost everyone?

00:29:22.377 --> 00:29:24.850
- Right, there will be
big decisions to be made

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:27.600
by governments as to what is privacy

00:29:27.600 --> 00:29:29.070
and what is collectively good.

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:32.220
And we've seen that over this
relatively minor pandemic,

00:29:32.220 --> 00:29:35.050
the big one is going to come
in our lifetimes, probably,

00:29:35.050 --> 00:29:36.800
as we wrote about in "Lifespan".

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:39.050
I'm on the front lines of this as well.

00:29:39.050 --> 00:29:40.310
I was building a company,

00:29:40.310 --> 00:29:42.060
have a company called Arc Bio,

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:44.520
that is able to detect viruses

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:47.730
and fungi and bacteria in human blood,

00:29:47.730 --> 00:29:49.480
and any sample, from a spit sample,

00:29:50.630 --> 00:29:53.430
in the effort to get ahead of COVID-19.

00:29:53.430 --> 00:29:55.090
Now, it came a little too
early, we weren't ready,

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:56.530
but there are patients now

00:29:56.530 --> 00:29:57.363
that are being monitored

00:29:57.363 --> 00:29:59.500
by Arc Bio's technology with a blood test,

00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:01.390
that sees everything in the body.

00:30:01.390 --> 00:30:04.240
And instead of pulling out
the human DNA from a hair,

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:08.090
what we do is we pull out the human DNA

00:30:08.090 --> 00:30:10.020
and see what's left behind.

00:30:10.020 --> 00:30:11.300
And those are the pathogens,

00:30:11.300 --> 00:30:14.610
and we can actually diagnose
an infectious disease

00:30:14.610 --> 00:30:16.500
without even knowing what to look for.

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:17.963
- There's a lot of implications here

00:30:17.963 --> 00:30:19.770
that once again, like, you know,

00:30:19.770 --> 00:30:22.640
we should be talking about
now, rather than waiting.

00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:25.470
If people are going to be engaged

00:30:25.470 --> 00:30:27.670
in anything about the internet of things

00:30:27.670 --> 00:30:30.420
or what some people are
calling the internet of body,

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:33.620
this is the thing that we
should really be focused on

00:30:33.620 --> 00:30:34.970
and talking about.

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:38.450
Because it's not just voluntary
patches that we're wearing,

00:30:38.450 --> 00:30:40.740
we're figuring out lots of different ways

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:42.380
to collect this information,

00:30:42.380 --> 00:30:44.010
it's going to get easier and easier,

00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:45.410
and a lot of people are going to do this,

00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:47.150
because it looks convenient.

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:49.670
- Yeah, well, let's go
through some of the sensors

00:30:49.670 --> 00:30:50.503
that are here now

00:30:50.503 --> 00:30:53.120
and ones that are just
about to come on the market.

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:56.020
Look at me, I'm wearing
the blood glucose monitor,

00:30:56.020 --> 00:30:58.060
which we've talked about, we love.

00:30:58.060 --> 00:30:59.960
I've got a ring that measures my sleep

00:30:59.960 --> 00:31:01.870
and my heart rate and my movement.

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:03.390
Really need to focus on sleep.

00:31:03.390 --> 00:31:06.230
This helps me, it makes
me cognizant of my sleep.

00:31:06.230 --> 00:31:08.480
I've got one of these
watches that does heart,

00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:10.930
it does movement, walking, exercise.

00:31:10.930 --> 00:31:12.740
So I'm already a bit of a cybernaut-

00:31:12.740 --> 00:31:13.930
- And you've got that one on your chest.

00:31:13.930 --> 00:31:15.147
- Well, don't forget, yeah,

00:31:15.147 --> 00:31:17.570
this one's not commercially
available, but will be soon.

00:31:17.570 --> 00:31:20.230
- Right, and you're doing the
InsideTracker blood draws,

00:31:20.230 --> 00:31:21.063
- Right.

00:31:21.063 --> 00:31:21.980
So I know a lot about my body

00:31:21.980 --> 00:31:23.930
and I've actually been getting healthier

00:31:23.930 --> 00:31:26.660
and fitter over the years
because of it, it's no accident,

00:31:26.660 --> 00:31:29.260
and I love what I'm able to do.

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:30.880
I'm a scientist and I can correct things

00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:32.770
when they're out of whack and not optimal,

00:31:32.770 --> 00:31:35.550
and I wouldn't be able to do
that if I didn't monitor it.

00:31:35.550 --> 00:31:37.080
But I'm excited about the other things

00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:39.580
I'll be able to monitor
right now I'm blind to.

00:31:39.580 --> 00:31:41.653
Speaking of which, contact lenses.

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:44.420
Contact lenses are one area

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:46.844
you can measure blood glucose levels.

00:31:46.844 --> 00:31:47.890
There are tooth sensors,

00:31:47.890 --> 00:31:49.990
you can measure bacteria in your mouth.

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:53.130
You can also, currently, through
Viome and other companies,

00:31:53.130 --> 00:31:53.963
I was talking

00:31:53.963 --> 00:31:57.380
to the CEO of Viome the
other day, Naveen Jain,

00:31:57.380 --> 00:31:59.350
he's got a colon cancer test

00:31:59.350 --> 00:32:01.320
that works better than
anything on the market,

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:03.130
but he also can see health

00:32:03.130 --> 00:32:06.100
and give you supplements that
will improve your health,

00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:07.750
based on what's in your gut,

00:32:07.750 --> 00:32:09.440
and that's going to be monitored as well.

00:32:09.440 --> 00:32:11.400
I'm not sure how, maybe in the toilet.

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:13.210
- That's a poop test?

00:32:13.210 --> 00:32:14.470
- Right now, you send off your poop,

00:32:14.470 --> 00:32:16.970
but eventually there'll be a
device in your home, I imagine.

00:32:16.970 --> 00:32:18.740
- Which is literally something
people are doing right now,

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:20.503
is they're sending their poop by mail to-

00:32:20.503 --> 00:32:22.540
- I've done it.
- Yeah.

00:32:22.540 --> 00:32:25.090
- Yeah, it felt weird going to the store

00:32:25.090 --> 00:32:26.250
with a box full of my poop,

00:32:26.250 --> 00:32:29.060
but I did it in the case
of a colon cancer test,

00:32:29.060 --> 00:32:31.710
which a lot of Australians
are doing just normally.

00:32:31.710 --> 00:32:32.690
But it is weird.

00:32:32.690 --> 00:32:36.233
- We should probably say,
it's not a whole poop.

00:32:37.190 --> 00:32:38.770
Wait, did you send a whole poop?

00:32:38.770 --> 00:32:40.240
- Well, what do you think it is?

00:32:40.240 --> 00:32:42.070
- It's just a swab.

00:32:42.070 --> 00:32:46.150
- I think it's a fairly
large piece of poop in a jar.

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:46.983
- Wow.

00:32:46.983 --> 00:32:48.640
Okay, so this is the
reality that we live in now.

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:49.740
- It was a big bottle,

00:32:50.833 --> 00:32:53.930
and then you put in liquid
and you shake, it's gross.

00:32:53.930 --> 00:32:56.050
But I learnt that I
didn't have colon cancer,

00:32:56.050 --> 00:32:56.883
so that was worth it.

00:32:56.883 --> 00:32:57.960
- It's probably worth it, right.

00:32:57.960 --> 00:32:58.845
And I think a of these...

00:32:58.845 --> 00:33:00.880
I mean, sort of like the
overarching theme here,

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:03.310
is that a lot of these
things come with trade offs.

00:33:03.310 --> 00:33:04.210
They come with the trade off

00:33:04.210 --> 00:33:06.500
for the potential for embarrassment,

00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:08.600
there are privacy implications.

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:09.840
We have to measure those trade offs

00:33:09.840 --> 00:33:13.030
against what we potentially can gain.

00:33:13.030 --> 00:33:18.030
Some of these other biosensors
are put into mouth guards.

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:19.730
- For lactate?

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:21.390
Or is it uric acid?

00:33:21.390 --> 00:33:22.930
- The mouth guard biosensors,

00:33:22.930 --> 00:33:24.140
I'm not sure what they're using.

00:33:24.140 --> 00:33:25.800
Although, I have heard them implicated

00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:27.920
in, like, youth athletics,

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:29.650
because when you have a concussion,

00:33:29.650 --> 00:33:33.590
you immediately start to
see markers in the blood.

00:33:33.590 --> 00:33:36.210
And so, presumably in the saliva too,

00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:37.320
those can test those.

00:33:37.320 --> 00:33:39.260
There's temporary tattoo biosensors,

00:33:39.260 --> 00:33:44.010
and there's also sweat
microfluidic biosensors

00:33:44.010 --> 00:33:47.470
that can detect all kinds of hormones.

00:33:47.470 --> 00:33:48.750
- Right, and then eventually,

00:33:48.750 --> 00:33:51.510
we'll have these optimized
athletes as well.

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:53.340
Already they're wearing a lot of devices,

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:56.970
but eventually they'll be
monitored for all sorts of things,

00:33:56.970 --> 00:33:58.680
like a Formula one racing car.

00:33:58.680 --> 00:34:00.730
- Well, but what you're suggesting though,

00:34:00.730 --> 00:34:03.090
is that eventually everyone will,

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:06.560
from pretty much every
age, be being monitored,

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:08.990
you said thousands of times each second.

00:34:08.990 --> 00:34:09.823
- Doesn't it make sense?

00:34:09.823 --> 00:34:10.656
It makes sense to me.

00:34:10.656 --> 00:34:12.290
I wouldn't want to walk around,

00:34:12.290 --> 00:34:14.180
potentially going to have
a heart attack tomorrow

00:34:14.180 --> 00:34:16.790
or having cancer grow on
me and not know about it.

00:34:16.790 --> 00:34:17.750
I want to know this stuff.

00:34:17.750 --> 00:34:19.880
And that's what I think
will lengthen our lives

00:34:19.880 --> 00:34:21.280
by another decade,

00:34:21.280 --> 00:34:24.780
beyond what we're able to do
with changes to lifestyle.

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:27.840
- Let's bring this
together with aging though,

00:34:27.840 --> 00:34:28.673
because so far,

00:34:28.673 --> 00:34:29.970
what we've really been talking about

00:34:29.970 --> 00:34:34.970
is the transitions that we
can see in our biochemistry

00:34:35.020 --> 00:34:38.100
that take us from states of
wellness to states of disease,

00:34:38.100 --> 00:34:39.560
that's really important.

00:34:39.560 --> 00:34:44.560
How aging fits into
this is also important.

00:34:45.220 --> 00:34:46.053
- Well, there are a number of ways

00:34:46.053 --> 00:34:47.660
to measure your biological age.

00:34:47.660 --> 00:34:49.860
I've used InsideTracker
for over a decade now

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:54.050
to steadily get ostensibly
younger biochemically,

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:55.123
over that decade.

00:34:56.150 --> 00:34:59.070
I was 48 and it spiked, my age went up,

00:34:59.070 --> 00:35:00.850
based on their inner age test.

00:35:00.850 --> 00:35:02.110
And then I changed my lifestyle,

00:35:02.110 --> 00:35:04.240
I started taking NMN, Metformin,

00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:05.680
and got that number, inner age,

00:35:05.680 --> 00:35:08.690
down to 31.4 in a matter of months.

00:35:08.690 --> 00:35:10.390
That doesn't mean I
was literally that age,

00:35:10.390 --> 00:35:12.520
just means that my blood
biochemistry markers

00:35:12.520 --> 00:35:15.850
matched somebody that was more like 34.

00:35:15.850 --> 00:35:20.850
- Right now, in order to have
your biological aging assessed

00:35:22.040 --> 00:35:24.640
through one of these methylation clocks,

00:35:24.640 --> 00:35:27.790
it's pretty expensive,
it's really time consuming,

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:30.490
it can take weeks, if not
months, to get results back.

00:35:30.490 --> 00:35:32.050
You're involved in an effort

00:35:32.050 --> 00:35:34.910
to try to bring down both the price point

00:35:34.910 --> 00:35:37.110
and the time period

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:39.660
that people have to wait to
learn their biological age.

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:41.390
- Yeah, these tests
aren't perfect right now,

00:35:41.390 --> 00:35:42.500
they're fairly expensive,

00:35:42.500 --> 00:35:43.860
they take weeks to get results.

00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:45.460
But that's going to change.

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:47.200
The student in my lab,
you know Patrick Griffin,

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:51.140
he spent his PhD trying to
figure out how to make it cheap.

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:52.200
And he figured it out,

00:35:52.200 --> 00:35:54.083
we've just put a paper online.

00:35:55.140 --> 00:35:56.870
Viewers can go and download that,

00:35:56.870 --> 00:35:59.660
it's at a site called bioRxiv,

00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:03.590
Sinclair, Griffin,
keyword TIME-Seq, S-E-Q.

00:36:03.590 --> 00:36:05.830
That paper is describing a way

00:36:05.830 --> 00:36:08.800
to do this methylation
clock for 1,000 times less.

00:36:08.800 --> 00:36:09.910
We're spinning out a company,

00:36:09.910 --> 00:36:12.430
we're in the process of
hopefully in the next few months,

00:36:12.430 --> 00:36:13.470
having a product available.

00:36:13.470 --> 00:36:15.590
So people can at home do a cheek swab

00:36:15.590 --> 00:36:17.580
and routinely measure
their biological age,

00:36:17.580 --> 00:36:18.413
which is important,

00:36:18.413 --> 00:36:21.810
because there are ways to
slow down and stop aging,

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:22.970
soon, reverse aging,

00:36:22.970 --> 00:36:23.803
and without that test,

00:36:23.803 --> 00:36:25.820
you won't know what's working for you.

00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:28.310
People can sign up, we
haven't got the product yet,

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:31.220
but there is a website,
it's doctorsinclair.com,

00:36:31.220 --> 00:36:33.570
spell out the word doctor,
doctorsinclair.com,

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:35.080
and you can get on a wait list.

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:36.430
Do that, because the more people we have,

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:37.840
the more experiments we can do together

00:36:37.840 --> 00:36:40.250
to figure out what slows
aging and even reverses it.

00:36:40.250 --> 00:36:43.080
- Once you have that number

00:36:43.080 --> 00:36:46.300
and bringing that together
with all of these other metrics

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:47.913
that we've been talking about,

00:36:48.823 --> 00:36:50.710
the measurements of the microbiome,

00:36:50.710 --> 00:36:52.753
the measurements of the blood analytes,

00:36:53.710 --> 00:36:54.840
all of the stuff that you're getting

00:36:54.840 --> 00:36:56.190
from, like, your smartwatch

00:36:56.190 --> 00:36:57.940
or whatever sensors you're wearing,

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:01.530
you can start making decisions
in your day to day life.

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:03.890
Let's talk about some of those.

00:37:03.890 --> 00:37:05.520
What does this look like

00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:08.170
in terms of the way that
we eat in the future?

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:10.120
'Cause what we've already said
is you've got to eat better,

00:37:10.120 --> 00:37:13.380
but this is eating better and
eating more informed as well.

00:37:13.380 --> 00:37:14.820
- Yeah, well there are
a couple of companies

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:18.255
that we're involved with,
and they support this show,

00:37:18.255 --> 00:37:19.390
that tell us what we can eat.

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:21.430
So Levels is telling us what's bad to eat

00:37:21.430 --> 00:37:24.200
for raising blood sugar
and what we should avoid.

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:26.220
The app, actually, we were
looking at this together,

00:37:26.220 --> 00:37:29.820
you can compare foods with
this app, which is fun.

00:37:29.820 --> 00:37:32.690
InsideTracker also provides
nutritional recommendations,

00:37:32.690 --> 00:37:33.810
you might want to...

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:35.280
If you're deficient in vitamin D,

00:37:35.280 --> 00:37:37.700
they send you off and you
should eat more salmon,

00:37:37.700 --> 00:37:39.600
they even have recipes.

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:41.610
Suffice it to say that
we're entering a world

00:37:41.610 --> 00:37:43.390
where you'll be able
to look at your phone,

00:37:43.390 --> 00:37:44.990
it'll tell you what things

00:37:44.990 --> 00:37:47.160
to buy at the shop, at the supermarket,

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:48.920
and even what local
restaurants you can go to

00:37:48.920 --> 00:37:51.520
and which meals you should
have to optimize your health.

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:52.760
- Once we have all these measurements,

00:37:52.760 --> 00:37:56.520
you can watch your biological
clock from week to week,

00:37:56.520 --> 00:37:59.180
you can watch your blood analytes go up

00:37:59.180 --> 00:38:03.116
and down within the
course of a single day.

00:38:03.116 --> 00:38:03.949
- Your heart.

00:38:03.949 --> 00:38:04.782
- You can watch your heart rate,

00:38:04.782 --> 00:38:05.615
you can watch all of these things.

00:38:05.615 --> 00:38:06.680
Once you have those things,

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:10.730
you can start making decisions
about the way that you eat,

00:38:10.730 --> 00:38:12.460
which is really one of the biggest ways

00:38:12.460 --> 00:38:13.550
that we can impact these.

00:38:13.550 --> 00:38:14.960
- Well, that's what I do.

00:38:14.960 --> 00:38:17.310
It's not just that you can, you should.

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:19.380
And the sort of decisions
that you can make

00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:21.330
are, okay, if you're
deficient in vitamin D,

00:38:21.330 --> 00:38:24.130
you can be directed towards
eating certain foods, like fish,

00:38:24.130 --> 00:38:25.510
that have vitamin D.

00:38:25.510 --> 00:38:28.460
Or if you have certain
high levels of cortisol,

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:31.880
reduce your stress, do meditation,
don't exercise so hard.

00:38:31.880 --> 00:38:33.670
These are tweaks to your life

00:38:33.670 --> 00:38:37.070
that otherwise you wouldn't
know that you needed to do.

00:38:37.070 --> 00:38:40.670
- And the apps that will sort of, like,

00:38:40.670 --> 00:38:43.470
help you along in this regimen,

00:38:43.470 --> 00:38:44.430
you're not going to be expected

00:38:44.430 --> 00:38:46.310
to know all of this stuff yourself,

00:38:46.310 --> 00:38:48.780
they're going to lead
you through the process.

00:38:48.780 --> 00:38:49.613
- Well, they do.

00:38:49.613 --> 00:38:51.740
Most of these apps are
taking a lot of data,

00:38:51.740 --> 00:38:53.720
it's often terabytes of data,

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:56.930
and distilling it down
into a few simple steps

00:38:56.930 --> 00:38:58.600
for you to do each day.

00:38:58.600 --> 00:39:00.030
- Even if you're doing everything right,

00:39:00.030 --> 00:39:01.750
you're eating the right way,

00:39:01.750 --> 00:39:05.600
there's still times
that these measurements

00:39:05.600 --> 00:39:07.947
are going to tell you, "Hey, actually,

00:39:07.947 --> 00:39:08.940
"you're missing out on this."

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:11.280
And so now, we're getting
into this conversation about,

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:13.870
like, perhaps supplementation as well.

00:39:13.870 --> 00:39:15.620
- Yeah, that's a key component.

00:39:15.620 --> 00:39:16.760
It's particularly important

00:39:16.760 --> 00:39:19.240
for people who are on specialized diets,

00:39:19.240 --> 00:39:21.120
say, if you're a vegan or a vegetarian,

00:39:21.120 --> 00:39:24.940
you're going to be lacking
in vitamin B12, probably,

00:39:24.940 --> 00:39:26.220
you may have low NAD levels,

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:27.750
because you're getting older,

00:39:27.750 --> 00:39:28.583
you measure those,

00:39:28.583 --> 00:39:31.100
and then you'll have
recommendations to supplement

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:34.120
and bring those levels
back to an optimal level.

00:39:34.120 --> 00:39:36.330
They'll probably recommend,
"Go get that brand."

00:39:36.330 --> 00:39:37.163
Or, "Go get that."

00:39:37.163 --> 00:39:38.020
Right now, it's very difficult,

00:39:38.020 --> 00:39:40.840
you don't know what brands to choose,

00:39:40.840 --> 00:39:43.310
I'm working on a way
to solve that as well.

00:39:43.310 --> 00:39:46.190
But ultimately, I think what's
going to happen is in our homes,

00:39:46.190 --> 00:39:47.040
we're going to have a machine

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:48.870
that can give us a poly pill

00:39:48.870 --> 00:39:50.680
with the minerals, the vitamins,

00:39:50.680 --> 00:39:53.170
maybe even the medicines that
we need, right on the spot.

00:39:53.170 --> 00:39:55.360
- Feels like a little compounding pharmacy

00:39:55.360 --> 00:39:56.810
right on your kitchen encounter.

00:39:56.810 --> 00:39:58.120
- And this isn't crazy stuff.

00:39:58.120 --> 00:39:59.470
Daniel Kraft, an MD,

00:39:59.470 --> 00:40:01.250
a good friend of mine
from Stanford University,

00:40:01.250 --> 00:40:02.083
has a company

00:40:02.083 --> 00:40:04.960
that's built a machine
that's not yet available,

00:40:04.960 --> 00:40:09.110
that does mix those minerals,
vitamins, and even medicines,

00:40:09.110 --> 00:40:11.080
right there on your kitchen
countertop every morning.

00:40:11.080 --> 00:40:13.290
- And the thing about this,

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:16.220
is that what we're talking
about then are, you know,

00:40:16.220 --> 00:40:21.220
pills that are created
specifically for a specific person,

00:40:21.290 --> 00:40:23.700
as opposed to what we get right
now when we go to the store,

00:40:23.700 --> 00:40:26.810
which is a capsule that is the size

00:40:26.810 --> 00:40:30.490
that some average person
needs, whatever that means.

00:40:30.490 --> 00:40:32.440
- Right, it's not even male
and female half the time,

00:40:32.440 --> 00:40:33.570
but even if it is,

00:40:33.570 --> 00:40:35.260
it's, "Oh, men take
this, women take that."

00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:37.710
Not even asking how much volume

00:40:37.710 --> 00:40:39.020
or how much does that person weigh.

00:40:39.020 --> 00:40:40.700
We can be, you know, some people,

00:40:40.700 --> 00:40:42.590
three times heavier than others.

00:40:42.590 --> 00:40:45.340
- Right, on the side of,
like, the Tylenol bottle,

00:40:45.340 --> 00:40:47.790
it says like adults and children.

00:40:47.790 --> 00:40:49.460
Like, there's nothing else to consider-

00:40:49.460 --> 00:40:50.293
- It's ridiculous.
- Right?

00:40:50.293 --> 00:40:52.390
- Right, but these pills
that are made in the home,

00:40:52.390 --> 00:40:56.040
they're going to be made
specially for you, for that day,

00:40:56.040 --> 00:40:59.410
for that time of day and
for what you've been eating.

00:40:59.410 --> 00:41:00.790
- And for your genome, too.

00:41:00.790 --> 00:41:01.623
- Exactly.
- Right?

00:41:01.623 --> 00:41:04.830
Because people have different DNA,

00:41:04.830 --> 00:41:07.740
process and metabolize different chemicals

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:09.410
in different ways.

00:41:09.410 --> 00:41:11.210
Once that's worked into this,

00:41:11.210 --> 00:41:13.220
we can really, really start to pinpoint

00:41:13.220 --> 00:41:14.590
what people actually need,

00:41:14.590 --> 00:41:17.300
as opposed to what most
people need most of the time.

00:41:17.300 --> 00:41:18.133
- Right.

00:41:18.133 --> 00:41:20.830
And actually, medicine
as it's currently called,

00:41:20.830 --> 00:41:22.710
is based on an average person.

00:41:22.710 --> 00:41:25.390
And so, those ranges that doctors look at

00:41:25.390 --> 00:41:26.450
to say, "Oh, you're sick.

00:41:26.450 --> 00:41:27.300
Or, "You're not."

00:41:27.300 --> 00:41:28.870
Are based on an average human

00:41:28.870 --> 00:41:32.140
and doesn't take into
account often your history,

00:41:32.140 --> 00:41:34.100
your genetics, what you've been eating,

00:41:34.100 --> 00:41:36.320
how biologically old you are.

00:41:36.320 --> 00:41:38.800
And these things should be
brought to bear as well.

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:41.090
And eventually, doctors
will have this information

00:41:41.090 --> 00:41:43.020
and all be assisted by AI.

00:41:43.020 --> 00:41:44.660
But right now, we're in
that transition zone.

00:41:44.660 --> 00:41:47.380
And interestingly, the AI
is being used by customers,

00:41:47.380 --> 00:41:48.740
not by doctors so much.

00:41:48.740 --> 00:41:50.470
- These tracking devices

00:41:50.470 --> 00:41:51.760
and the measurements that they give us,

00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:53.390
are also going to help us understand

00:41:53.390 --> 00:41:56.350
how to exercise more effectively

00:41:56.350 --> 00:41:58.430
in a way that will help us

00:41:58.430 --> 00:42:00.430
not just bring down
these individual levels,

00:42:00.430 --> 00:42:03.610
but also presumably bring down
our biological age over time.

00:42:03.610 --> 00:42:05.250
- Already, we can do that.

00:42:05.250 --> 00:42:08.210
The ring that I'm wearing,
the wristband, this patch,

00:42:08.210 --> 00:42:10.150
they're measuring my
heart rate constantly.

00:42:10.150 --> 00:42:11.630
They're looking at heart rate variability,

00:42:11.630 --> 00:42:13.220
which you want to be high.

00:42:13.220 --> 00:42:15.330
They're also looking
at resting heart rate,

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:17.133
which you want to be low in general.

00:42:18.050 --> 00:42:19.670
There are certain ways to improve that,

00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:21.580
there's running, there's weights,

00:42:21.580 --> 00:42:24.290
there's yoga, even meditation.

00:42:24.290 --> 00:42:26.640
The apps will soon be so sophisticated,

00:42:26.640 --> 00:42:28.997
they'll say, "Okay, today you
should go on this run here.

00:42:28.997 --> 00:42:29.987
"I'll send you on a map.

00:42:29.987 --> 00:42:30.820
"This'll be nice.

00:42:30.820 --> 00:42:31.840
"It's good weather."

00:42:31.840 --> 00:42:33.777
Versus, "Oh, maybe today you need a rest.

00:42:33.777 --> 00:42:35.647
"You've got a bit of cortisol.

00:42:35.647 --> 00:42:38.210
"You ran yesterday, do
some weights today."

00:42:38.210 --> 00:42:40.590
And that is going to be
optimized, in addition,

00:42:40.590 --> 00:42:41.423
with the supplements.

00:42:41.423 --> 00:42:43.617
They're going to say, "Okay,
don't take this supplement today,

00:42:43.617 --> 00:42:46.070
"because I'm going to tell
you to go lift weights."

00:42:46.070 --> 00:42:47.740
- I imagine this world where, like,

00:42:47.740 --> 00:42:49.950
Siri becomes your personal trainer.

00:42:49.950 --> 00:42:52.970
- Pretty much you're going to
have a guardian angel for health,

00:42:52.970 --> 00:42:53.810
and when that happens,

00:42:53.810 --> 00:42:55.080
you're going to look at the days

00:42:55.080 --> 00:42:57.460
when we went for an annual physical

00:42:57.460 --> 00:42:59.610
as though those days are medieval.

00:42:59.610 --> 00:43:02.840
- There's not going to be a
one size fits all solution

00:43:02.840 --> 00:43:04.490
for everybody when it comes to exercise,

00:43:04.490 --> 00:43:06.440
just like diet and supplementation, right?

00:43:06.440 --> 00:43:07.350
Everybody's going to be different.

00:43:07.350 --> 00:43:09.200
- Well clearly, and I
say this all the time,

00:43:09.200 --> 00:43:10.507
when I give talks, people ask me,

00:43:10.507 --> 00:43:11.340
"Well, what should I do?

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:13.840
"Tell me what you do,
David, and I'll do that."

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:15.350
Well, that's what people should do,

00:43:15.350 --> 00:43:16.880
because everybody's different,

00:43:16.880 --> 00:43:20.170
genetically, physically, even mentally.

00:43:20.170 --> 00:43:22.720
What I can do, skip meals,
some people cannot do.

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:26.550
So even though on, as
I often say, page 304,

00:43:26.550 --> 00:43:28.300
I listed what I do in the book,

00:43:28.300 --> 00:43:30.230
that's not the perfect prescription

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:31.200
for everybody, of course,

00:43:31.200 --> 00:43:32.580
because everyone's different.

00:43:32.580 --> 00:43:34.330
So you've got to measure
yourself like I do

00:43:34.330 --> 00:43:36.870
and see what works for
you and what doesn't.

00:43:36.870 --> 00:43:39.363
- Before we get off the
subject of exercise,

00:43:40.551 --> 00:43:41.430
I wanted to talk a little bit

00:43:41.430 --> 00:43:43.400
about this idea of exercise mimetics.

00:43:43.400 --> 00:43:48.400
Right now we know,
particularly in animal studies,

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:49.890
the mice in your lab

00:43:49.890 --> 00:43:54.890
are getting an exercise benefit
out of consumption of NMN.

00:43:55.400 --> 00:43:58.530
But NMN is a pretty rudimentary molecule,

00:43:58.530 --> 00:44:02.160
it's not a designer, it's pretty simple.

00:44:02.160 --> 00:44:04.810
There are new drugs coming down the line,

00:44:04.810 --> 00:44:07.470
as we're talking about the future,

00:44:07.470 --> 00:44:10.110
is there a point in
time in which, you know,

00:44:10.110 --> 00:44:12.250
these supplements, these drugs,

00:44:12.250 --> 00:44:13.320
are going to allow us

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:17.810
to be our slovenly selves
without consequence?

00:44:17.810 --> 00:44:19.920
- Well, we're not there yet.

00:44:19.920 --> 00:44:22.040
And in fact, what the
data says from my lab,

00:44:22.040 --> 00:44:24.550
is that when you give
NMN and you exercise,

00:44:24.550 --> 00:44:25.690
you get the best benefit.

00:44:25.690 --> 00:44:28.510
So it's not an excuse to sit
around and just pop a pill,

00:44:28.510 --> 00:44:31.200
but there is a need for better medicines.

00:44:31.200 --> 00:44:32.780
The reason that I'm developing,

00:44:32.780 --> 00:44:35.810
at one of my companies,
a better NMN version,

00:44:35.810 --> 00:44:37.370
is that the elderly

00:44:37.370 --> 00:44:39.260
and people who just came
in with a heart attack

00:44:39.260 --> 00:44:40.680
or have kidney failure,

00:44:40.680 --> 00:44:43.790
they cannot be on a fasting regimen easily

00:44:43.790 --> 00:44:45.460
or asked to go for a run.

00:44:45.460 --> 00:44:47.000
So you do need to be able to deliver these

00:44:47.000 --> 00:44:48.620
with a pill or with IV.

00:44:48.620 --> 00:44:50.380
But for those of us who are still fit,

00:44:50.380 --> 00:44:51.920
the best bang for the buck,

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:53.860
at least with today's technology,

00:44:53.860 --> 00:44:56.910
is to eat right, move right,

00:44:56.910 --> 00:45:00.670
and supplement in a way that
you can see by monitoring,

00:45:00.670 --> 00:45:03.030
what's optimizing your body for longevity.

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:06.700
- Ultimately, one of the places

00:45:06.700 --> 00:45:10.300
that you think we may wind up,

00:45:10.300 --> 00:45:15.300
is a world in which we can reset the clock

00:45:15.410 --> 00:45:17.443
through a course of treatments.

00:45:18.460 --> 00:45:21.080
And you've done experiments
about this in your lab,

00:45:21.080 --> 00:45:22.420
you've had quite a bit of success

00:45:22.420 --> 00:45:24.040
with mice in doing this,

00:45:24.040 --> 00:45:29.040
and this is what the Fahy
trials seem to be showing

00:45:29.980 --> 00:45:31.460
in Southern California.

00:45:31.460 --> 00:45:33.080
Let's dive into that,

00:45:33.080 --> 00:45:35.980
because this is not immediate,

00:45:35.980 --> 00:45:39.680
but it is likely down the road somewhere

00:45:39.680 --> 00:45:40.950
in the fairly near future.

00:45:40.950 --> 00:45:42.200
- Well, so Greg Fahy,

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:44.680
the professor out in California

00:45:44.680 --> 00:45:45.720
who's doing this with patients

00:45:45.720 --> 00:45:47.850
and published with Steve Horvath,

00:45:47.850 --> 00:45:50.470
they're seeing a couple
of years retreat in age

00:45:50.470 --> 00:45:52.330
in 12 months of treatment,

00:45:52.330 --> 00:45:54.560
with a combination of three factors

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:56.450
that we discussed in an earlier episode.

00:45:56.450 --> 00:45:57.500
But this is just the beginning,

00:45:57.500 --> 00:46:00.210
this is the early days of flight,

00:46:00.210 --> 00:46:01.770
we're just learning how this works,

00:46:01.770 --> 00:46:03.430
but very rapidly over the next few years,

00:46:03.430 --> 00:46:06.100
we're going to learn what else
can reverse the age of the body,

00:46:06.100 --> 00:46:07.010
certainly the blood clock,

00:46:07.010 --> 00:46:08.980
which is what's being used right now.

00:46:08.980 --> 00:46:10.800
And there are reports
of people going back 10,

00:46:10.800 --> 00:46:13.060
even 20 years with certain treatments.

00:46:13.060 --> 00:46:15.390
So this is a golden
age for aging research.

00:46:15.390 --> 00:46:19.150
I didn't realize when I
started out in my career,

00:46:19.150 --> 00:46:21.640
I didn't expect to be
talking about age reversal,

00:46:21.640 --> 00:46:23.727
I thought, "Oh yeah, we'll,
we'll slow down aging

00:46:23.727 --> 00:46:26.000
"and that'll be the best, if we're lucky."

00:46:26.000 --> 00:46:28.850
We're in a world, that for me,
is science fiction already,

00:46:28.850 --> 00:46:31.420
we're way beyond my expectations,
which is super exciting.

00:46:31.420 --> 00:46:32.253
- The other thing that you're working on,

00:46:32.253 --> 00:46:35.050
and I think a lot of people
are really excited about,

00:46:35.050 --> 00:46:39.670
is this idea of an epigenetic
reset of biological age.

00:46:39.670 --> 00:46:42.913
This is stuff that you're
doing in your lab right now.

00:46:44.160 --> 00:46:48.460
Let's talk about taking mice

00:46:48.460 --> 00:46:50.570
and reversing their biological age.

00:46:50.570 --> 00:46:51.403
- Right.

00:46:51.403 --> 00:46:52.579
So we're talking about a gene therapy.

00:46:52.579 --> 00:46:55.430
We found that there are three genes

00:46:55.430 --> 00:46:58.380
that are normally turned on in
embryos that keep them young,

00:46:58.380 --> 00:47:01.530
that we could turn on
again in the adult animal

00:47:01.530 --> 00:47:02.710
and in human cells.

00:47:02.710 --> 00:47:04.400
So it works in both species.

00:47:04.400 --> 00:47:05.233
But in the mice,

00:47:05.233 --> 00:47:07.500
we could manipulate
them to see what happens

00:47:07.500 --> 00:47:10.270
when you rejuvenate their
tissues using these three genes.

00:47:10.270 --> 00:47:11.890
What we-
- And the genes are?

00:47:11.890 --> 00:47:15.220
- Well, the they're called oct
four, SOCS two and KLF four.

00:47:15.220 --> 00:47:17.490
These are what are called
transcription factors,

00:47:17.490 --> 00:47:20.860
proteins that bind to DNA
and turn on genetic programs.

00:47:20.860 --> 00:47:22.610
- And they're part of the series of genes

00:47:22.610 --> 00:47:25.370
that people call the Yamanaka factors.

00:47:25.370 --> 00:47:26.261
- Yes.

00:47:26.261 --> 00:47:28.270
So Shinya Yamanaka from Japan,

00:47:28.270 --> 00:47:31.880
won the Nobel Prize in 2016 for
the discovery of five genes,

00:47:31.880 --> 00:47:34.170
three of which I've just
mentioned, there are two others,

00:47:34.170 --> 00:47:37.630
that were able to take
cells, an adult cell,

00:47:37.630 --> 00:47:40.470
back to a pluripotent stem cell stage,

00:47:40.470 --> 00:47:42.210
like basically age zero.

00:47:42.210 --> 00:47:43.043
And then, you could...

00:47:43.043 --> 00:47:46.860
We do use these cells
to build other tissues,

00:47:46.860 --> 00:47:48.650
they can be turned into
nerve cells and muscle.

00:47:48.650 --> 00:47:50.640
But you don't want to do that in the body,

00:47:50.640 --> 00:47:53.530
you'll become the world's largest tumor.

00:47:53.530 --> 00:47:55.850
So we found that a combination
of three of these genes

00:47:55.850 --> 00:47:57.900
and leaving out the
other two dangerous ones,

00:47:57.900 --> 00:48:00.120
was a perfect way to
reset the age of the body,

00:48:00.120 --> 00:48:01.700
without causing cancer.

00:48:01.700 --> 00:48:03.900
And it worked better than
we could even predict it.

00:48:03.900 --> 00:48:07.010
We put these genes, using a virus,

00:48:07.010 --> 00:48:08.130
into the eye, for example,

00:48:08.130 --> 00:48:09.350
we could do the whole body as well,

00:48:09.350 --> 00:48:10.760
but let's focus on the eye.

00:48:10.760 --> 00:48:12.630
- You did into the eye of a mouse.

00:48:12.630 --> 00:48:14.120
- It's a mouse-
- Yeah.

00:48:14.120 --> 00:48:15.340
- We hope in a couple of years,

00:48:15.340 --> 00:48:17.600
we'll be testing our first human,

00:48:17.600 --> 00:48:18.690
and right now, we've done mice,

00:48:18.690 --> 00:48:20.450
we're now doing non-human primates.

00:48:20.450 --> 00:48:21.283
So we're getting closer.

00:48:21.283 --> 00:48:23.490
But in the mice, it was
just a stunning result,

00:48:23.490 --> 00:48:26.790
which is a mouse that's
had its optic nerve damaged

00:48:26.790 --> 00:48:28.040
or a mouse that has glaucoma,

00:48:28.040 --> 00:48:30.440
which is pressure in the
eye that causes blindness,

00:48:30.440 --> 00:48:32.250
it affects millions of
people around the world,

00:48:32.250 --> 00:48:33.090
or just old age,

00:48:33.090 --> 00:48:35.180
these mice were essentially blind.

00:48:35.180 --> 00:48:37.390
We then put the virus in
with these three genes,

00:48:37.390 --> 00:48:41.350
could turn on those three
genes, O, S and K for short,

00:48:41.350 --> 00:48:45.070
and four weeks later, those
cells, the retinal cells,

00:48:45.070 --> 00:48:47.890
the nerve cells that go to
the brain, went back in time,

00:48:47.890 --> 00:48:49.920
they became young again, reset their age,

00:48:49.920 --> 00:48:51.780
according to the Horvath clock.

00:48:51.780 --> 00:48:53.010
Their gene expression patterns,

00:48:53.010 --> 00:48:55.392
which means the genes
that were dysregulated,

00:48:55.392 --> 00:48:58.710
went back to being regulated
perfectly for a nerve cell,

00:48:58.710 --> 00:49:01.080
and the mice got their vision back.

00:49:01.080 --> 00:49:03.510
We actually cured blindness in those mice,

00:49:03.510 --> 00:49:04.600
which was a stunning result.

00:49:04.600 --> 00:49:07.210
We also showed that the
systems that reset the clock,

00:49:07.210 --> 00:49:11.300
there are enzymes that control
DNA methylation called TEDS.

00:49:11.300 --> 00:49:14.140
Without those, we didn't
get vision restoration,

00:49:14.140 --> 00:49:15.520
and the clock didn't go back.

00:49:15.520 --> 00:49:16.360
So what that tells us

00:49:16.360 --> 00:49:19.600
is the clock going backwards is doable.

00:49:19.600 --> 00:49:22.360
there is a reset switch in the body,

00:49:22.360 --> 00:49:25.130
and that the clock is part of that system.

00:49:25.130 --> 00:49:25.963
- And this all sounds...

00:49:25.963 --> 00:49:27.970
I know, to people who
haven't heard about it,

00:49:27.970 --> 00:49:31.580
at first it sounds sort
of futuristic and crazy,

00:49:31.580 --> 00:49:34.930
this was the cover story
on the journal, Nature.

00:49:34.930 --> 00:49:36.040
- Yeah, December, 2020,

00:49:36.040 --> 00:49:39.560
we were fortunate enough
to get the cover's issue,

00:49:39.560 --> 00:49:41.210
it's a dream for any scientist.

00:49:41.210 --> 00:49:43.030
And the title that they had on the cover

00:49:43.030 --> 00:49:44.510
was "Turning Back Time"

00:49:44.510 --> 00:49:47.300
and a picture of an eyeball
with a clock hand going.

00:49:47.300 --> 00:49:48.980
Yeah, that was one of the
highlights of my career,

00:49:48.980 --> 00:49:51.420
and what's happened since
is there's been a whirlwind

00:49:51.420 --> 00:49:54.520
of interest in this from
scientists around the world.

00:49:54.520 --> 00:49:57.097
Investors, there's more
than 20 billion with a B,

00:49:57.097 --> 00:49:59.629
$20 billion being raised

00:49:59.629 --> 00:50:03.380
to look at this actual
phenomenon of aging reset,

00:50:03.380 --> 00:50:05.590
and a lot of scientists
are turning their attention

00:50:05.590 --> 00:50:08.230
towards understanding how this works,

00:50:08.230 --> 00:50:11.650
and even mimicking this gene
therapy with little molecules,

00:50:11.650 --> 00:50:12.740
perhaps plant molecules

00:50:12.740 --> 00:50:14.940
or drugs that are on the market already,

00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:17.870
so that one day, hopefully
in the next five to 10 years,

00:50:17.870 --> 00:50:20.510
we could take a pill or
rub a cream on our skin,

00:50:20.510 --> 00:50:21.460
not just to slow aging,

00:50:21.460 --> 00:50:23.510
but to truly reset the age of the body.

00:50:23.510 --> 00:50:26.600
- Between then and now,

00:50:26.600 --> 00:50:29.273
there's obviously a lot of work to do.

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:33.310
We've seen this effect
in the eyes of mice,

00:50:33.310 --> 00:50:36.730
you're working on other
systems within a mouse as well.

00:50:36.730 --> 00:50:38.440
- Well, we are, and in
humans, I should say,

00:50:38.440 --> 00:50:40.680
we're trying to go as quickly as we can.

00:50:40.680 --> 00:50:43.890
We've found that the reset
works in other parts of the eye,

00:50:43.890 --> 00:50:46.070
we can restore vision in mice

00:50:46.070 --> 00:50:49.140
that have what's called
retinal degeneration

00:50:49.140 --> 00:50:50.570
or macular degeneration.

00:50:50.570 --> 00:50:54.210
We also have skin reversal
for humans and mice,

00:50:54.210 --> 00:50:56.480
and we've even built little mini brains,

00:50:56.480 --> 00:50:58.860
we grow these little
organoids out of human skin,

00:50:58.860 --> 00:51:00.270
we give them Alzheimer's disease,

00:51:00.270 --> 00:51:02.980
these little brains get dementia,

00:51:02.980 --> 00:51:05.110
and we can reset the
age of those mini brains

00:51:05.110 --> 00:51:07.480
and they get their
electrical activity back.

00:51:07.480 --> 00:51:09.180
So this is the beginnings of work

00:51:09.180 --> 00:51:10.180
that I hope will mean

00:51:10.180 --> 00:51:13.930
that we can reverse the age of
the brain and other tissues,

00:51:13.930 --> 00:51:15.870
and diseases of old age,

00:51:15.870 --> 00:51:20.290
like Alzheimer's, heart disease,
even cancer, will go away.

00:51:20.290 --> 00:51:23.010
- What this would look like
in practice, presumably,

00:51:23.010 --> 00:51:24.020
at some time in the future,

00:51:24.020 --> 00:51:25.690
would be a course of treatment, right?

00:51:25.690 --> 00:51:28.940
Like, you have experienced
some degree of aging,

00:51:28.940 --> 00:51:31.790
you decide, "Hey, it's time
for me to take care of this."

00:51:31.790 --> 00:51:35.290
You go in, you have this gene therapy,

00:51:35.290 --> 00:51:37.300
and then these genes
are basically turned on

00:51:37.300 --> 00:51:40.250
inside your body to do their work.

00:51:40.250 --> 00:51:41.083
- That's right.

00:51:41.083 --> 00:51:42.130
And we built the system and the virus,

00:51:42.130 --> 00:51:45.450
so we could turn it on with
an antibiotic, doxycyclin.

00:51:45.450 --> 00:51:48.360
And the patients that we
treat, if we treat them,

00:51:48.360 --> 00:51:50.080
will take a course of antibiotics

00:51:50.080 --> 00:51:51.210
to turn on the reprogramming.

00:51:51.210 --> 00:51:54.570
So we can turn it on for,
say, four to eight weeks.

00:51:54.570 --> 00:51:55.950
We can measure their vision.

00:51:55.950 --> 00:51:57.800
When they get their vision back again,

00:51:57.800 --> 00:51:59.660
they stop the antibiotics.

00:51:59.660 --> 00:52:02.330
Then, they age out again,
maybe another decade,

00:52:02.330 --> 00:52:03.850
and then all they get in the mail

00:52:03.850 --> 00:52:05.110
is another course of antibiotics

00:52:05.110 --> 00:52:06.640
to restore their vision again.

00:52:06.640 --> 00:52:07.581
- A lot of people

00:52:07.581 --> 00:52:08.790
are going to be really
concerned by this idea

00:52:08.790 --> 00:52:11.430
of, like, gene therapy,

00:52:11.430 --> 00:52:13.400
but there are, as you noted, like,

00:52:13.400 --> 00:52:17.060
some other avenues that we
might get a very similar result.

00:52:17.060 --> 00:52:21.358
Greg Fahy's work is
aimed at this same idea

00:52:21.358 --> 00:52:25.500
of an epigenetic reprogramming
without the genetic therapy.

00:52:25.500 --> 00:52:28.670
He's doing this through some
pretty simple chemicals.

00:52:28.670 --> 00:52:33.320
- Yeah, so he's in California
and he used three treatments.

00:52:33.320 --> 00:52:35.440
It's called the TRIIM Trial

00:52:35.440 --> 00:52:37.210
and it included Metformin,

00:52:37.210 --> 00:52:40.240
growth hormone and DHEA, another hormone.

00:52:40.240 --> 00:52:43.720
And so, growth hormone
will rebuild tissues,

00:52:43.720 --> 00:52:46.570
and that gives a response in
the body that times are good,

00:52:46.570 --> 00:52:49.530
but he also counteracted
that with adversity mimetics,

00:52:49.530 --> 00:52:52.490
the Metformin and the DHEA
prevented glucose spikes

00:52:52.490 --> 00:52:54.620
and type-two diabetes.

00:52:54.620 --> 00:52:56.890
And what he showed after
a year of this treatment

00:52:56.890 --> 00:52:59.320
was that the epigenetic
clock measured by Horvath,

00:52:59.320 --> 00:53:02.280
remember, this is the
DNA methylation patterns,

00:53:02.280 --> 00:53:04.910
reset in a way that was calculated

00:53:04.910 --> 00:53:07.320
to take their blood biomarkers back,

00:53:07.320 --> 00:53:10.210
their blood clock back two
and a half years on average.

00:53:10.210 --> 00:53:12.070
Now, that doesn't seem like a lot.

00:53:12.070 --> 00:53:14.090
- That was after one year of treatment.

00:53:14.090 --> 00:53:18.810
One year of treatment on this
course of these three drugs,

00:53:18.810 --> 00:53:21.350
bought two and a half years
in equivalent biological age?

00:53:21.350 --> 00:53:22.487
- Yeah, and at first I said,

00:53:22.487 --> 00:53:24.140
"Two and a half years,
come on, that's not much."

00:53:24.140 --> 00:53:27.820
But then I realized if
you do that every year

00:53:27.820 --> 00:53:30.820
or even every two years, you're immortal,

00:53:30.820 --> 00:53:32.270
and that's when things
get really interesting.

00:53:32.270 --> 00:53:33.103
But what we don't know

00:53:33.103 --> 00:53:36.160
is are these treatments just
changing the blood clock

00:53:36.160 --> 00:53:37.500
or are they changing the whole body?

00:53:37.500 --> 00:53:39.200
So we need to measure other things,

00:53:39.200 --> 00:53:43.420
including skin clocks
and blood biomarkers,

00:53:43.420 --> 00:53:45.850
to really know if this
is a true aging reset.

00:53:45.850 --> 00:53:47.210
- So there's a bunch of ways

00:53:47.210 --> 00:53:49.210
that we're trying to
approach the same goal,

00:53:49.210 --> 00:53:54.210
which is this, like,
repeatable resetting of age.

00:53:55.400 --> 00:53:56.233
As of right now,

00:53:56.233 --> 00:53:57.900
we don't know how many
times it's repeatable,

00:53:57.900 --> 00:54:00.220
whether you can do it
once or twice or 10 times

00:54:00.220 --> 00:54:02.050
or however many times we want?

00:54:02.050 --> 00:54:04.600
- We don't know, but I
was speaking with Greg,

00:54:04.600 --> 00:54:05.433
who's a friend of mine,

00:54:05.433 --> 00:54:08.200
he said that he's been
repeating this treatment.

00:54:08.200 --> 00:54:11.150
And, you know, often when
these publications come out,

00:54:11.150 --> 00:54:13.860
we scientists know a lot more
than we're telling people.

00:54:13.860 --> 00:54:14.930
And what he told me,

00:54:14.930 --> 00:54:17.320
which I'll share with
all the audience today

00:54:17.320 --> 00:54:20.620
is it works multiple times.

00:54:20.620 --> 00:54:23.560
So he's now getting
people to go back five,

00:54:23.560 --> 00:54:25.840
and there are some reports
of people going back 10 years

00:54:25.840 --> 00:54:28.070
in their blood's biological age.

00:54:28.070 --> 00:54:30.710
- What do we need to know,

00:54:30.710 --> 00:54:33.220
to know whether that's
safe in the long-term?

00:54:33.220 --> 00:54:35.690
And what do we need to
know to know whether or not

00:54:35.690 --> 00:54:37.840
that can be continued in the long-term?

00:54:37.840 --> 00:54:40.350
There's not some drop off at some point,

00:54:40.350 --> 00:54:43.010
where it, like, just is
not going to work anymore.

00:54:43.010 --> 00:54:44.790
- Yeah, we don't know,
this is new territory,

00:54:44.790 --> 00:54:46.090
and I think that's a
really important point,

00:54:46.090 --> 00:54:49.000
is we don't know the dangers
of resetting the age.

00:54:49.000 --> 00:54:50.460
We believe that it's safe.

00:54:50.460 --> 00:54:53.920
So far, there's been no
indication of any downside.

00:54:53.920 --> 00:54:57.910
But one of the big worries is
if you go back too far in age,

00:54:57.910 --> 00:54:59.700
you can stimulate cancer cells.

00:54:59.700 --> 00:55:01.700
So that's another thing to watch out for.

00:55:01.700 --> 00:55:03.210
But I'm an optimist.

00:55:03.210 --> 00:55:05.560
I'm optimistic that if
the body becomes younger,

00:55:05.560 --> 00:55:09.250
it'll surveil the body
and kill the cancer cells,

00:55:09.250 --> 00:55:11.080
which is one of the
problems as we get older

00:55:11.080 --> 00:55:12.570
and why tumors develop,

00:55:12.570 --> 00:55:14.610
is that our body's immune system

00:55:14.610 --> 00:55:16.840
doesn't kill those cancer cells.

00:55:16.840 --> 00:55:18.740
But, you know, obviously we
have a lot of work to do,

00:55:18.740 --> 00:55:21.403
we're not saying go out
and try gene therapy today.

00:55:22.670 --> 00:55:25.130
We could do that theoretically,
but definitely don't,

00:55:25.130 --> 00:55:26.810
we don't know all the risks,

00:55:26.810 --> 00:55:29.990
and particularly with gene
therapy, it can be irreversible.

00:55:29.990 --> 00:55:31.490
So this is something...

00:55:31.490 --> 00:55:32.700
- Yeah, once you do it-
- Don't try this at home.

00:55:32.700 --> 00:55:33.660
- You're committed.

00:55:33.660 --> 00:55:36.570
- Right, and there can be
other side effects as well,

00:55:36.570 --> 00:55:37.403
from gene therapy,

00:55:37.403 --> 00:55:40.170
including immune responses
to those viruses.

00:55:40.170 --> 00:55:41.430
One word about those viruses

00:55:41.430 --> 00:55:45.010
is that it's still early
days on gene delivery.

00:55:45.010 --> 00:55:48.330
One of the reasons we haven't
immediately had success

00:55:48.330 --> 00:55:50.060
with extending the lifespan of mice,

00:55:50.060 --> 00:55:51.900
is that those viruses are very hard

00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:54.360
to distribute evenly through the body.

00:55:54.360 --> 00:55:55.560
Even in even humans,

00:55:55.560 --> 00:55:57.150
it's a struggle for diseases

00:55:57.150 --> 00:55:59.890
that want to be treated holistically.

00:55:59.890 --> 00:56:00.990
But there are advances,

00:56:00.990 --> 00:56:03.220
there're a number of new
companies that have sprung up

00:56:03.220 --> 00:56:06.600
that allow these viruses to
infect the body more evenly.

00:56:06.600 --> 00:56:07.433
And when that happens,

00:56:07.433 --> 00:56:09.053
I think we will be able to make mice

00:56:09.053 --> 00:56:11.140
that can live a decade or two.

00:56:11.140 --> 00:56:12.220
- So if we could take a mouse

00:56:12.220 --> 00:56:15.090
that right now has a
lifespan of about two years,

00:56:15.090 --> 00:56:17.950
and we can make it live a decade or two,

00:56:17.950 --> 00:56:19.330
and just playing

00:56:19.330 --> 00:56:21.340
with this idea of optimism
for a moment here,

00:56:21.340 --> 00:56:22.173
and sort of, like,

00:56:22.173 --> 00:56:24.630
setting aside all of
the things that, like,

00:56:24.630 --> 00:56:26.570
we don't know yet,

00:56:26.570 --> 00:56:30.670
those kinds of impacts on a human life,

00:56:30.670 --> 00:56:33.550
puts us out well past the 100 year marker,

00:56:33.550 --> 00:56:35.910
well past the 120 year marker.

00:56:35.910 --> 00:56:37.580
We start getting into territory

00:56:37.580 --> 00:56:38.480
that a lot of people

00:56:38.480 --> 00:56:41.750
just have a really hard
time even fathoming.

00:56:41.750 --> 00:56:44.130
But again, just playing
with that for a moment here,

00:56:44.130 --> 00:56:47.450
what we're talking about in the long-term,

00:56:47.450 --> 00:56:48.710
is a world where people

00:56:48.710 --> 00:56:51.640
are living these incredibly longer lives,

00:56:51.640 --> 00:56:54.720
presumably hopefully healthier as well.

00:56:54.720 --> 00:56:55.920
A lot of people,

00:56:55.920 --> 00:56:58.770
the first thing that they
hear when they hear that

00:56:58.770 --> 00:57:03.770
is more people on an already
really crowded planet.

00:57:03.780 --> 00:57:05.440
You hear this a lot from people.

00:57:05.440 --> 00:57:06.273
- Well, I do,

00:57:06.273 --> 00:57:08.500
even when we talk about
extending lifespan by five years,

00:57:08.500 --> 00:57:10.870
people wonder what are we going
to do with all these people?

00:57:10.870 --> 00:57:13.310
And the good news is, if
you do the calculations,

00:57:13.310 --> 00:57:15.640
first of all, most advanced countries,

00:57:15.640 --> 00:57:17.270
economically advanced,

00:57:17.270 --> 00:57:20.090
the rates of population growth
are starting to decline,

00:57:20.090 --> 00:57:21.499
even here in the US, it's coming down-

00:57:21.499 --> 00:57:22.970
- 'Cause people are having fewer children

00:57:22.970 --> 00:57:26.220
over the last few generations
as these countries get richer.

00:57:26.220 --> 00:57:28.610
- Yeah, richer leads to fewer offspring.

00:57:28.610 --> 00:57:30.880
Wealth, health, these all lead

00:57:30.880 --> 00:57:32.480
to people making decisions that,

00:57:33.397 --> 00:57:36.270
"I don't need to have 3, 5, 10 kids."

00:57:36.270 --> 00:57:37.710
And this is increasingly happening

00:57:37.710 --> 00:57:40.310
in South America and in Africa as well.

00:57:40.310 --> 00:57:42.570
And the prediction is that humanity

00:57:42.570 --> 00:57:46.160
will peak at about 10 billion, maybe 11,

00:57:46.160 --> 00:57:47.670
and start to come down again,

00:57:47.670 --> 00:57:50.030
and that's going to be when these changes

00:57:50.030 --> 00:57:51.090
actually happen to humanity,

00:57:51.090 --> 00:57:52.850
that people will live longer

00:57:52.850 --> 00:57:54.800
and there will not be an excess of people,

00:57:54.800 --> 00:57:56.180
it's not that we'll be overcrowded,

00:57:56.180 --> 00:57:58.140
that's clear from the math.

00:57:58.140 --> 00:57:59.230
The other concern people have

00:57:59.230 --> 00:58:01.050
is what about all the resources?

00:58:01.050 --> 00:58:03.300
And clearly, we need to figure out ways

00:58:03.300 --> 00:58:05.400
to preserve the planet,
preserve the environment,

00:58:05.400 --> 00:58:08.730
and not destroy it with
our over consumption.

00:58:08.730 --> 00:58:11.680
And one of the solutions that
often people don't think about

00:58:11.680 --> 00:58:15.920
is that these changes to human biology

00:58:15.920 --> 00:58:18.050
will allow people to
be much more productive

00:58:18.050 --> 00:58:20.220
and less costly to the economy.

00:58:20.220 --> 00:58:23.010
Right now, we throw away 17%

00:58:23.010 --> 00:58:26.570
of our GDP in the US on healthcare.

00:58:26.570 --> 00:58:27.630
Well, we don't throw it away,

00:58:27.630 --> 00:58:31.260
but a lot of that is used in
the last few years of life,

00:58:31.260 --> 00:58:34.860
which we can probably
fix with this technology.

00:58:34.860 --> 00:58:37.690
We've calculated that if
we could extend lifespan

00:58:37.690 --> 00:58:40.740
by just one year, using
Metformin as an example,

00:58:40.740 --> 00:58:43.240
which probably could
work better than that,

00:58:43.240 --> 00:58:46.563
the value to the US economy in
the long run is $86 trillion.

00:58:47.500 --> 00:58:50.240
And if you do it for 10
years, it's 365 trillion,

00:58:50.240 --> 00:58:52.953
we just published this in
the journal, Nature Aging.

00:58:53.860 --> 00:58:56.730
That is money that can be
put towards other problems,

00:58:56.730 --> 00:58:58.040
improving education,

00:58:58.040 --> 00:59:01.190
helping to combat climate
change, among other things,

00:59:01.190 --> 00:59:04.330
making the world much more
efficient and less wasteful.

00:59:04.330 --> 00:59:05.713
- We've get to use that
money wisely, though.

00:59:05.713 --> 00:59:07.720
I mean, like, there was this sense

00:59:07.720 --> 00:59:08.640
at the end of the cold war

00:59:08.640 --> 00:59:10.040
that we were going to
have this peace dividend,

00:59:10.040 --> 00:59:12.100
and all this money that
we were spending on war,

00:59:12.100 --> 00:59:13.360
we were now going to be able to spend

00:59:13.360 --> 00:59:14.760
on all these social programs

00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:16.230
and making people's lives better,

00:59:16.230 --> 00:59:19.000
and we just threw it
back into the military.

00:59:19.000 --> 00:59:23.070
There's also a possibility
that we misuse this.

00:59:23.070 --> 00:59:24.970
So this is another instance

00:59:24.970 --> 00:59:27.910
where we really need to start
having these conversations now

00:59:27.910 --> 00:59:30.530
and really working them
into the conversations

00:59:30.530 --> 00:59:33.833
and making sure that policy
makers understand the world

00:59:33.833 --> 00:59:36.310
that we're potentially entering into.

00:59:36.310 --> 00:59:37.143
- For sure.

00:59:37.143 --> 00:59:38.940
But the sums are astronomical.

00:59:38.940 --> 00:59:40.620
Already, we've got more money

00:59:40.620 --> 00:59:43.850
being put into reprogramming
and age correction,

00:59:43.850 --> 00:59:45.010
epigenetic reprogramming,

00:59:45.010 --> 00:59:47.130
than ever in any other
time in medical history.

00:59:47.130 --> 00:59:49.900
This is a true historical event

00:59:49.900 --> 00:59:50.860
that we're experiencing here.

00:59:50.860 --> 00:59:53.080
It's one of the reasons we
wanted to do this podcast

00:59:53.080 --> 00:59:56.023
is to bring people along as
these amazing changes happen.

00:59:56.940 --> 00:59:59.730
But also, the sums of
money that we'll be saving

00:59:59.730 --> 01:00:01.317
are also unprecedented.

01:00:01.317 --> 01:00:03.750
$360 trillion just in the US,

01:00:03.750 --> 01:00:07.760
that's money that, yeah,
even if some is wasted,

01:00:07.760 --> 01:00:10.240
there's going to be a lot
to use for good purposes.

01:00:10.240 --> 01:00:13.970
But I think what we want to
do is educate Congress people,

01:00:13.970 --> 01:00:16.550
that when this money comes,

01:00:16.550 --> 01:00:18.370
it's used for the right purposes.

01:00:18.370 --> 01:00:21.340
- And that's on the healthcare
saving side of things,

01:00:21.340 --> 01:00:24.670
which tends to be the later
in life side of things.

01:00:24.670 --> 01:00:26.850
There's another advantage to this too,

01:00:26.850 --> 01:00:28.610
is that if we can
lengthen lifespans, right?

01:00:28.610 --> 01:00:30.180
The portion of a human life

01:00:30.180 --> 01:00:32.290
that we spend educating someone right now

01:00:32.290 --> 01:00:33.810
is about a quarter of their life, right?

01:00:33.810 --> 01:00:36.410
It's the first 20 years of
their life, give or take.

01:00:37.520 --> 01:00:39.930
And then you get, you know, two decades,

01:00:39.930 --> 01:00:43.220
or two equivalent time periods
where they work, right?

01:00:43.220 --> 01:00:45.750
So you get about a 40 year career,

01:00:45.750 --> 01:00:46.583
and then you get a 20 year period

01:00:46.583 --> 01:00:48.480
at the end of their
life where they're sick.

01:00:48.480 --> 01:00:50.960
But if you can extend out that time

01:00:50.960 --> 01:00:52.210
in which they are being productive,

01:00:52.210 --> 01:00:55.050
all of a sudden all of
those educational costs

01:00:55.050 --> 01:00:57.620
become even better investments.

01:00:57.620 --> 01:00:58.453
And they already are,

01:00:58.453 --> 01:01:00.730
and as you and I both agree I know,

01:01:00.730 --> 01:01:03.550
education is the best investment
we can make in our society.

01:01:03.550 --> 01:01:05.070
- Right, and not everybody starts out life

01:01:05.070 --> 01:01:06.560
with a career that they want.

01:01:06.560 --> 01:01:07.677
They don't have the opportunity,

01:01:07.677 --> 01:01:09.050
and so with a longer life,

01:01:09.050 --> 01:01:11.990
there should be multiple
opportunities to change careers

01:01:11.990 --> 01:01:15.260
and do things that people
have always wanted to do.

01:01:15.260 --> 01:01:17.487
But to make that possible,
we advocate a skill-batical,

01:01:17.487 --> 01:01:19.510
a term that we coined.

01:01:19.510 --> 01:01:21.410
This would be two or
three years paid time off

01:01:21.410 --> 01:01:23.210
to change careers,

01:01:23.210 --> 01:01:24.860
to do something you always wanted.

01:01:24.860 --> 01:01:25.750
Everyone should have that opportunity.

01:01:25.750 --> 01:01:27.270
- Throughout the course of people's lives.

01:01:27.270 --> 01:01:29.920
- Yeah, as lives get longer and
more productive and healthy,

01:01:29.920 --> 01:01:31.970
there should be multiple
opportunities to change

01:01:31.970 --> 01:01:34.990
and do, ultimately, what people
have always wanted to do.

01:01:34.990 --> 01:01:37.870
My father is a good example, he's now 82.

01:01:37.870 --> 01:01:42.650
He left his main career
in his late 60s, 67,

01:01:42.650 --> 01:01:44.567
and he thought he was going
to die in 10 years from then

01:01:44.567 --> 01:01:47.000
and it wasn't going to be very pretty.

01:01:47.000 --> 01:01:49.530
He's now 82, he's as
healthy as he ever was.

01:01:49.530 --> 01:01:51.210
He's stronger than me.

01:01:51.210 --> 01:01:53.730
He started a new career in his 70s.

01:01:53.730 --> 01:01:56.610
This is the kind of life
that I want for everybody,

01:01:56.610 --> 01:01:58.510
that we don't have to
worry about getting cancer

01:01:58.510 --> 01:02:01.910
in our 50s and 60s or having
a heart attack in our 70s.

01:02:01.910 --> 01:02:03.090
We should be able to live in our 80s,

01:02:03.090 --> 01:02:05.550
90s and beyond in a healthy way,

01:02:05.550 --> 01:02:08.070
contributing to society,
educating the community,

01:02:08.070 --> 01:02:10.130
educating our kids, our grandkids,

01:02:10.130 --> 01:02:11.790
even our great grandkids
with all the wisdom

01:02:11.790 --> 01:02:13.860
that we accumulate over our lifetimes.

01:02:13.860 --> 01:02:17.520
It's a huge waste when
somebody 80, 90 or 100 dies.

01:02:17.520 --> 01:02:19.550
- Well, it's such a waste that,

01:02:19.550 --> 01:02:20.850
we've argued about this before,

01:02:20.850 --> 01:02:22.880
you've suggested that people

01:02:22.880 --> 01:02:24.790
don't just have a right to live that long,

01:02:24.790 --> 01:02:26.060
they really have a responsibility

01:02:26.060 --> 01:02:27.010
and an obligation to live that long-

01:02:27.010 --> 01:02:28.780
- Well, they do-
- And healthy.

01:02:28.780 --> 01:02:29.660
- Well, it's important.

01:02:29.660 --> 01:02:32.150
Most people who are
over the age of 40 or 50

01:02:32.150 --> 01:02:34.640
have taken care of a
grandparent or a parent,

01:02:34.640 --> 01:02:35.980
who's gotten sick.

01:02:35.980 --> 01:02:38.210
It's a huge burden on everybody,

01:02:38.210 --> 01:02:40.150
and it's not just bad for
the person who's dying,

01:02:40.150 --> 01:02:43.530
it's bad for the family,
it's costly, time consuming,

01:02:43.530 --> 01:02:46.710
and emotionally draining to say the least.

01:02:46.710 --> 01:02:47.543
What we're talking about

01:02:47.543 --> 01:02:50.170
is take responsibility for yourself,

01:02:50.170 --> 01:02:52.820
but also do it for your kids
and your grandkids sake,

01:02:52.820 --> 01:02:54.550
because if you live a long time

01:02:54.550 --> 01:02:57.120
and are not a burden on your family

01:02:57.120 --> 01:02:58.270
and you live a long time,

01:02:58.270 --> 01:03:01.560
you'll probably die much
quicker, that's the statistic,

01:03:01.560 --> 01:03:03.220
